      "headline": "Invention of the Compass",
      "text": "The compass was invented in China during the Han Dynasty, around the 2nd century BCE. It was initially used for divination and later adapted for navigation.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Use of Compass for Navigation",
      "text": "By the 11th century, the compass was being used for navigation by the Chinese. This marked a significant advancement in maritime exploration.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Compass to Europe",
      "text": "The compass was introduced to Europe in the late 12th century, likely through contact with the Arab world, which had adopted it from the Chinese.",
      "date": "1190"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Dry Compass",
      "text": "In the 13th century, the dry compass was developed in Europe. This version used a pivoting needle over a compass card.",
      "date": "1300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Invention of the Liquid Compass",
      "text": "The liquid compass was invented in the early 20th century. It improved stability and accuracy by using a liquid-filled chamber to dampen needle movement.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Electronic Compasses",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, electronic compasses were developed. These devices use magnetometers and other technologies to determine direction.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration of GPS Technology",
      "text": "In the 21st century, GPS technology was integrated into compasses, providing precise location and direction information.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

