      "headline": "Introduction of the Term 'Dysthymia'",
      "text": "The term 'dysthymia' was introduced by Robert Spitzer in the late 1970s as a replacement for the term 'depressive personality'. This marked a significant shift in the understanding and classification of mood disorders.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inclusion in DSM-III",
      "text": "Dysthymia was included as a distinct mood disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Third Edition (DSM-III), published by the American Psychiatric Association in 1980.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reclassification as Persistent Depressive Disorder",
      "text": "In 2013, with the publication of the DSM-5, dysthymia was reclassified as persistent depressive disorder (PDD), combining dysthymic disorder and chronic major depressive disorder into a single diagnosis.",
      "date": "2013"
    }
  ]
}

{
