      "text": "In 1609, the Twelve Years' Truce was signed between the Dutch Republic and Spain, temporarily halting hostilities during the Eighty Years' War.",
      "date": "1609"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dutch Golden Age",
      "text": "The 17th century marked the Dutch Golden Age, a period of great wealth, cultural achievement, and maritime expansion for the Dutch Republic.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peace of Westphalia",
      "text": "On October 24, 1648, the Peace of Westphalia was signed, ending the Eighty Years' War and recognizing the independence of the Dutch Republic.",
      "date": "1648-10-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rampjaar (Disaster Year)",
      "text": "In 1672, the Dutch Republic faced a crisis known as the Rampjaar, when it was simultaneously invaded by France, England, and two German states.",
      "date": "1672"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Glorious Revolution",
      "text": "In 1688, William III of Orange, a stadtholder of the Dutch Republic, became King of England, Scotland, and Ireland, in the event known as the Glorious Revolution.",
      "date": "1688"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fourth Anglo-Dutch War",
      "text": "The Fourth Anglo-Dutch War took place from 1780 to 1784, resulting in a defeat for the Dutch Republic and weakening its global influence.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Batavian Revolution",
      "text": "In 1795, the Batavian Revolution occurred, leading to the end of the Dutch Republic and the establishment of the Batavian Republic under French influence.",
      "date": "1795"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Positron",
      "text": "In 1932, Carl D. Anderson discovered the positron, the antiparticle of the electron, while studying cosmic rays. This was the first known particle of antimatter.",
      "date": "1932"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dirac's Theory of Antimatter",
      "text": "In 1928, Paul Dirac developed a theoretical framework that predicted the existence of antimatter. His equations suggested that every particle has a corresponding antiparticle.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Artificial Production of Antimatter",
      "text": "In 1955, antiprotons were first produced at the Bevatron particle accelerator at the University of California, Berkeley. This marked the first artificial production of antimatter.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of Antihydrogen",
      "text": "In 1995, scientists at CERN created the first atoms of antihydrogen, consisting of a positron orbiting an antiproton.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trapping of Antihydrogen Atoms",
      "text": "In 2010, CERN's ALPHA experiment successfully trapped antihydrogen atoms for the first time, allowing for detailed study of antimatter.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Measurement of Antimatter Spectrum",
      "text": "In 2016, the ALPHA experiment at CERN made the first measurement of the optical spectrum of an antimatter atom, comparing it to hydrogen.",
      "date": "2016"
    }
  ]
}

