      "text": "In the 1770s, the term 'Semitic' was first used by members of the Göttingen school of history. This terminology was derived from Shem, one of the three sons of Noah in the Book of Genesis, and was used to categorize ethnic, cultural, or racial groups associated with people of the Middle East.",
      "date": "1770"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Concept of Semitic Languages",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th century, the concept of Semitic languages was developed as a linguistic classification. This grouping includes languages such as Arabic, Hebrew, Aramaic, and Akkadian.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shift in Usage of 'Semitic' Term",
      "text": "By the 20th century, the term 'Semitic' became largely unused outside the context of linguistics, specifically in the grouping of Semitic languages. The term's racial and ethnic connotations were largely abandoned.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

