      "text": "In The Myth of Sisyphus, Camus introduces his philosophy of the absurd, which lies in the juxtaposition between the human need for meaning and the universe's unreasonable silence.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Camus' Argument Against Suicide",
      "text": "Camus argues that the realization of the absurd does not justify suicide. Instead, it requires a 'revolt' against the absurdity of life.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Comparison with Sisyphus",
      "text": "In the final chapter, Camus compares the absurdity of man's life with the myth of Sisyphus, who is condemned to eternally push a boulder up a mountain, only for it to roll down each time.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conclusion of The Myth of Sisyphus",
      "text": "The essay concludes with the idea that 'The struggle itself towards the heights is enough to fill a man's heart. One must imagine Sisyphus happy.'",
      "date": "1942"
    }
  ]
}

