      "headline": "Birth of Vito Genovese",
      "text": "Vito Genovese was born on November 21, 1897, in Risigliano, a frazione of Tufino, in the Province of Naples, Italy.",
      "date": "1897-11-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Immigration to the United States",
      "text": "In 1913, Vito Genovese immigrated to the United States with his family, settling in Little Italy, Manhattan, New York City.",
      "date": "1913"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Involvement in Organized Crime",
      "text": "By the 1920s, Genovese became involved in organized crime, working with Charles 'Lucky' Luciano and other prominent mobsters.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Participation in the Castellammarese War",
      "text": "During the early 1930s, Genovese took part in the Castellammarese War, a violent power struggle for control of the Italian-American Mafia.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Role in the Murder of Joe Masseria",
      "text": "On April 15, 1931, Genovese was involved in the murder of Joe Masseria, which helped end the Castellammarese War and led to the rise of Luciano as a major Mafia leader.",
      "date": "1931-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fleeing to Italy",
      "text": "In 1937, facing charges of murder, Genovese fled to Italy to avoid prosecution.",
      "date": "1937"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to the United States",
      "text": "Genovese returned to the United States in 1945 after World War II, when the charges against him were dropped due to lack of evidence.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becoming Boss of the Luciano Crime Family",
      "text": "In 1957, Genovese became the boss of the Luciano crime family, which was later renamed the Genovese crime family by the FBI.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest and Conviction",
      "text": "In 1959, Genovese was convicted of narcotics trafficking and sentenced to 15 years in prison.",
      "date": "1959"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Vito Genovese",
      "text": "Vito Genovese died of a heart attack on February 14, 1969, while still incarcerated at the United States Medical Center for Federal Prisoners in Springfield, Missouri.",
      "date": "1969-02-14"
    }
  ]
