      "headline": "Birth of Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab",
      "text": "Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab was born in 1703 in the region of Najd, in present-day Saudi Arabia.",
      "date": "1703"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab's Education",
      "text": "During the early 18th century, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab traveled to various Islamic centers of learning, including Mecca and Medina, to study Islamic jurisprudence and theology.",
      "date": "1720"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to Najd and Preaching",
      "text": "In the 1740s, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab returned to Najd and began preaching his reformist ideas, emphasizing a return to the pure monotheism of early Islam and opposing practices he viewed as innovations.",
      "date": "1740"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Alliance with Muhammad ibn Saud",
      "text": "In 1744, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab formed a pact with the local ruler Muhammad ibn Saud, which led to the establishment of a political-religious alliance that would become the foundation of the first Saudi state.",
      "date": "1744"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab",
      "text": "Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab died in 1792, but his teachings continued to influence the region and beyond.",
      "date": "1792"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Saudi State",
      "text": "The first Saudi state, based on Wahhabist principles, expanded throughout the Arabian Peninsula during the late 18th and early 19th centuries.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ottoman-Saudi War",
      "text": "In 1818, the Ottoman Empire, with the help of Egyptian forces, defeated the first Saudi state, capturing its capital, Diriyah.",
      "date": "1818"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Second Saudi State",
      "text": "In 1824, the second Saudi state was established, continuing to promote Wahhabism as its religious foundation.",
      "date": "1824"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Foundation of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia",
      "text": "In 1932, Abdulaziz Ibn Saud unified the Arabian Peninsula and established the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, with Wahhabism as the dominant religious doctrine.",
      "date": "1932"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of Wahhabism",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, Wahhabism spread beyond Saudi Arabia, influencing Islamic movements in various parts of the world.",
      "date": "1950"
    }
  ]
}

