      "headline": "Creation of the Prisoner's Dilemma",
      "text": "The prisoner's dilemma was designed by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher in 1950 during their work at the RAND Corporation.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Experiment with Armen Alchian and John Williams",
      "text": "Economist Armen Alchian and mathematician John Williams were invited to play a hundred rounds of the prisoner's dilemma game. They often chose to cooperate, providing early insights into cooperative behavior in repeated games.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Nash's Insight",
      "text": "John Nash remarked that rational behavior in the iterated version of the prisoner's dilemma can differ from that in a single-round version. This insight anticipated a key result in game theory: cooperation can emerge in repeated interactions.",
      "date": "1950"
    }
  ]
}

