      "text": "Sumo is believed to have originated in ancient Japan as a form of ritual dance to entertain the Shinto deities. The earliest recorded mention of sumo is in the Kojiki, a chronicle of early Japanese history, which dates back to the early 8th century.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Sumo Matches",
      "text": "The first sumo matches were held in the Nara period as part of Shinto rituals. These matches were often violent and could result in the death of one of the participants.",
      "date": "0729"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Sumo Rules",
      "text": "During the Heian period, sumo began to develop into a more organized sport with established rules. Matches were held at the imperial court and were used as a form of military training.",
      "date": "0794"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sumo as a Professional Sport",
      "text": "In the Edo period, sumo became a professional sport with the establishment of the first sumo stables and the introduction of the ranking system. Sumo tournaments began to be held regularly in Edo (now Tokyo).",
      "date": "1603"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Japan Sumo Association",
      "text": "The Japan Sumo Association was formed in 1925 to oversee the professional sumo tournaments and maintain the traditions of the sport.",
      "date": "1925"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Six-Tournament System",
      "text": "In 1958, the Japan Sumo Association introduced the current system of six official tournaments (honbasho) held each year.",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Foreign Yokozuna",
      "text": "In 1993, Akebono Tarō, born Chadwick Haheo Rowan in Hawaii, became the first foreign-born yokozuna, the highest rank in sumo.",
      "date": "1993"
    }
  ]
