      "headline": "Coining of the Term 'Banana Republic'",
      "text": "In 1904, American author O. Henry coined the term 'banana republic' to describe countries like Guatemala and Honduras, which were economically exploited by U.S. corporations such as the United Fruit Company.",
      "date": "1904"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Exploitation by U.S. Corporations",
      "text": "During the early 20th century, U.S. corporations, notably the United Fruit Company, heavily influenced the economies and politics of Central American countries, leading to the establishment of banana republics.",
      "date": "1900-1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Characteristics of a Banana Republic",
      "text": "A banana republic typically features a society with extremely stratified social classes, including a large impoverished working class and a ruling class plutocracy composed of business, political, and military elites.",
      "date": "1900-1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exploitation of Labor and Resources",
      "text": "The ruling class in a banana republic controls the primary sector of the economy through the exploitation of labor, particularly in large-scale plantation agriculture like banana cultivation.",
      "date": "1900-1920"
    }
  ]
}

