      "headline": "Triangle Shirtwaist Factory Fire",
      "text": "On March 25, 1911, a fire broke out at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory in the Greenwich Village neighborhood of Manhattan, New York City. It was the deadliest industrial disaster in the city's history, resulting in the deaths of 146 garment workers, including 123 women and girls and 23 men. Most victims were recent Italian or Jewish immigrants aged 14 to 23.",
      "date": "1911-03-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on Labor Laws",
      "text": "The Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire led to significant changes in labor laws and safety regulations in the United States. It spurred the growth of the International Ladies' Garment Workers' Union and led to the establishment of the Factory Investigating Commission, which pushed for reforms in workplace safety standards.",
      "date": "1911"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trial of Factory Owners",
      "text": "In December 1911, the factory owners, Max Blanck and Isaac Harris, were tried for manslaughter. They were acquitted, but the trial raised public awareness about the need for better safety standards in factories.",
      "date": "1911-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legacy and Commemoration",
      "text": "The Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire is commemorated annually to honor the victims and to remind the public of the importance of workplace safety. It remains a pivotal event in the history of labor rights in the United States.",
      "date": "1911"
    }
  ]
}

