    {
      "headline": "René Descartes Coins the Term 'Imaginary'",
      "text": "In 1637, French philosopher and mathematician René Descartes coined the term 'imaginary' to describe numbers that were not real, in his work 'La Géométrie'.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal Definition of Complex Numbers",
      "text": "In the 18th century, mathematicians like Euler and d'Alembert began to formalize the concept of complex numbers, recognizing them as pairs of real numbers.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Caspar Wessel's Geometric Interpretation",
      "text": "In 1799, Norwegian-Danish mathematician Caspar Wessel provided a geometric interpretation of complex numbers as points in the complex plane, which laid the groundwork for the modern understanding of complex numbers.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carl Friedrich Gauss's Contributions",
      "text": "In 1831, Carl Friedrich Gauss further developed the theory of complex numbers, introducing the term 'complex number' and promoting their acceptance in mathematical analysis.",
      "date": "1831"
    },
    {
      "headline": "William Rowan Hamilton's Work on Quaternions",
      "text": "In 1843, Irish mathematician William Rowan Hamilton extended the concept of complex numbers to higher dimensions with his discovery of quaternions.",
      "date": "1843"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Complex Analysis",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th century, complex analysis, the study of functions of complex variables, became a major area of mathematical research, with contributions from mathematicians like Augustin-Louis Cauchy and Bernhard Riemann.",
      "date": "1800"
