      "headline": "Historical Extraction of Sodium Carbonate",
      "text": "Historically, sodium carbonate was extracted from the ashes of plants grown in sodium-rich soils. This method was used for centuries before the development of industrial processes.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Leblanc Process",
      "text": "In the late 18th century, the Leblanc process was developed in France. This industrial process allowed for the production of sodium carbonate from sodium chloride (salt) and sulfuric acid, marking a significant advancement in chemical manufacturing.",
      "date": "1791"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Solvay Process",
      "text": "The Solvay process was developed by Ernest Solvay in 1861. This method became the dominant industrial process for producing sodium carbonate, using sodium chloride and limestone.",
      "date": "1861"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chloralkali Process Development",
      "text": "The chloralkali process, which involves the electrolysis of sodium chloride solution to produce sodium hydroxide, chlorine, and hydrogen, was developed in the late 19th century. Sodium hydroxide can be carbonated to produce sodium carbonate.",
      "date": "1890"
    }
  ]
}

