      "headline": "Formulation of the First Law of Thermodynamics",
      "text": "The First Law of Thermodynamics, also known as the law of energy conservation, was formulated in the early 19th century. It states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another.",
      "date": "1824"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formulation of the Second Law of Thermodynamics",
      "text": "The Second Law of Thermodynamics, which introduces the concept of entropy, was formulated in the mid-19th century. It states that the total entropy of an isolated system can never decrease over time.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Third Law of Thermodynamics",
      "text": "The Third Law of Thermodynamics was formulated by Walther Nernst in 1906. It states that as the temperature of a system approaches absolute zero, the entropy of the system approaches a constant minimum.",
      "date": "1906"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formulation of the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics",
      "text": "The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics was formulated in the early 20th century. It states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, then they are in thermal equilibrium with each other.",
      "date": "1931"
    }
  ]
}

