      "headline": "Tutor to William Cavendish",
      "text": "In 1608, Hobbes became the tutor to William Cavendish, later the 2nd Earl of Devonshire, marking the beginning of his long association with the Cavendish family.",
      "date": "1608"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Translation of Thucydides",
      "text": "In 1629, Hobbes published a translation of Thucydides' 'History of the Peloponnesian War', his first major work.",
      "date": "1629"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'De Cive'",
      "text": "In 1642, Hobbes published 'De Cive', a work that laid the groundwork for his later political theories.",
      "date": "1642"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Leviathan'",
      "text": "Hobbes published 'Leviathan' in 1651, his most famous work, which established the foundation for most of Western political philosophy.",
      "date": "1651"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to England",
      "text": "In 1651, after the publication of 'Leviathan', Hobbes returned to England from Paris, where he had been living in exile.",
      "date": "1651"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'De Corpore'",
      "text": "In 1655, Hobbes published 'De Corpore', a work on metaphysics and philosophy of science.",
      "date": "1655"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'De Homine'",
      "text": "In 1658, Hobbes published 'De Homine', which focused on human nature and behavior.",
      "date": "1658"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Thomas Hobbes",
      "text": "Thomas Hobbes died on December 4, 1679, in Derbyshire, England.",
      "date": "1679-12-04"
