      "headline": "Identification by Sigmund Freud",
      "text": "The Madonna–whore complex was first identified by Sigmund Freud, who referred to it as psychic impotence. Freud's work on this complex highlighted the dichotomy in which men categorize women as either saintly Madonnas or debased whores.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Freud's Description",
      "text": "Sigmund Freud wrote about the Madonna–whore complex, stating, 'Where such men love they have no desire, and where they desire they cannot love.' This description encapsulates the psychological struggle faced by men with this complex.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Uwe Hartmann's Analysis",
      "text": "In 2009, clinical psychologist Uwe Hartmann wrote that the Madonna–whore complex 'is still highly prevalent in today's patients,' indicating the ongoing relevance and impact of this psychological complex in modern times.",
      "date": "2009"
    }
  ]
}

