      "headline": "Creation of the Agile Manifesto",
      "text": "In February 2001, 17 software developers met at the Snowbird resort in Utah to discuss lightweight development methods. This meeting resulted in the creation of the Manifesto for Agile Software Development, which emphasized values such as individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan.",
      "date": "2001-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Agile Alliance",
      "text": "Following the creation of the Agile Manifesto in 2001, the Agile Alliance was formed. This non-profit organization was established to promote the principles of agile software development and to support practitioners in adopting agile practices.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Agile Manifesto",
      "text": "The Agile Manifesto was published online in 2001, making its principles and values widely accessible to software developers and organizations around the world. This publication marked a significant shift in software development methodologies.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Agile Practices in Industry",
      "text": "Throughout the early 2000s, agile practices such as Scrum, Extreme Programming (XP), and Kanban began to be adopted by software development teams across various industries. These practices emphasized iterative development, collaboration, and flexibility.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Agile Becomes Mainstream",
      "text": "By the 2010s, agile methodologies had become mainstream in software development. Many organizations adopted agile practices to improve their development processes, increase flexibility, and better respond to customer needs.",
      "date": "2010s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Agile Beyond Software Development",
      "text": "In the 2010s and beyond, the principles of agile development began to be applied beyond software development, influencing areas such as project management, product development, and organizational change.",
      "date": "2010s"
    }
  ]
}

