      "headline": "Pierre de Fermat's Conjecture",
      "text": "In 1637, Pierre de Fermat wrote in the margin of his copy of 'Arithmetica' that he had discovered a truly marvelous proof of this proposition, which this margin is too narrow to contain. This statement became known as Fermat's Last Theorem.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Euler's Contribution",
      "text": "In 1738, Leonhard Euler provided a proof for the case n = 3, which was one of the first significant steps towards proving Fermat's Last Theorem.",
      "date": "1738"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sophie Germain's Work",
      "text": "In the early 19th century, Sophie Germain developed a strategy to prove the theorem for a large class of prime exponents, making significant progress.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ernst Kummer's Work",
      "text": "In 1847, Ernst Kummer proved the theorem for a large class of prime exponents using his theory of ideal numbers.",
      "date": "1847"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Andrew Wiles' Proof",
      "text": "On June 23, 1993, Andrew Wiles announced a proof of Fermat's Last Theorem at a lecture in Cambridge, England. His proof was based on the modularity theorem for semistable elliptic curves.",
      "date": "1993-06-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Wiles' Proof",
      "text": "In 1995, after correcting an error in the initial proof, Andrew Wiles' proof of Fermat's Last Theorem was published in the journal 'Annals of Mathematics'.",
      "date": "1995"
    }
  ]
}

