      "text": "The first use of lethal injection as a method of execution was carried out in the United States on December 7, 1982, when Charles Brooks Jr. was executed in Texas.",
      "date": "1982-12-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Lethal Injection Protocol",
      "text": "The concept of lethal injection was first proposed in 1977 by Dr. Jay Chapman, the state medical examiner of Oklahoma, as a more humane method of execution compared to electrocution or gas chamber.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by U.S. States",
      "text": "By the mid-1980s, several U.S. states had adopted lethal injection as their primary method of execution, following Texas's lead.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Supreme Court Ruling on Lethal Injection",
      "text": "In 2008, the U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of lethal injection in the case Baze v. Rees, ruling that it did not constitute cruel and unusual punishment.",
      "date": "2008"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Controversy Over Drug Availability",
      "text": "In the 2010s, pharmaceutical companies began restricting the sale of drugs used in lethal injections, leading to shortages and forcing states to seek alternative drugs or methods.",
      "date": "2010s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Botched Executions and Legal Challenges",
      "text": "Throughout the 2010s and into the 2020s, several botched executions using lethal injection led to increased legal challenges and scrutiny over the method's humaneness.",
      "date": "2010s-2020s"
    }
  ]
}

