      "text": "On January 25, 1959, Pope John XXIII announced his intention to convene the Second Vatican Council, aiming to address relations between the Roman Catholic Church and the modern world.",
      "date": "1959-01-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Opening of the Second Vatican Council",
      "text": "The Second Vatican Council officially opened on October 11, 1962, under the pontificate of Pope John XXIII. The council was held in Saint Peter's Basilica in Vatican City.",
      "date": "1962-10-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Session of the Council",
      "text": "The first session of the Second Vatican Council took place from October 11, 1962, to December 8, 1962. This session focused on setting the agenda and procedures for the council.",
      "date": "1962-10-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Pope John XXIII",
      "text": "Pope John XXIII, who convened the Second Vatican Council, died on June 3, 1963. His successor, Pope Paul VI, continued the council.",
      "date": "1963-06-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Session of the Council",
      "text": "The second session of the Second Vatican Council was held from September 29, 1963, to December 4, 1963. During this session, the council began to produce significant documents.",
      "date": "1963-09-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Session of the Council",
      "text": "The third session of the Second Vatican Council took place from September 14, 1964, to November 21, 1964. Key documents such as 'Lumen Gentium' were promulgated during this session.",
      "date": "1964-09-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fourth and Final Session of the Council",
      "text": "The fourth and final session of the Second Vatican Council occurred from September 14, 1965, to December 8, 1965. The council concluded with the promulgation of several important documents, including 'Gaudium et Spes'.",
      "date": "1965-09-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Closing of the Second Vatican Council",
      "text": "The Second Vatican Council officially closed on December 8, 1965. It resulted in significant changes in the Catholic Church's approach to liturgy, ecumenism, and its relationship with the modern world.",
      "date": "1965-12-08"
    }
  ]
}

