      "headline": "Introduction of the Term 'Carcinisation'",
      "text": "The term 'carcinisation' was introduced into evolutionary biology by L. A. Borradaile. He described it as 'the many attempts of Nature to evolve a crab'.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carcinisation in Anomura",
      "text": "Carcinisation is notably observed in the infraorder Anomura, which includes hermit crabs, king crabs, and others. Over evolutionary time, some species within this group have independently evolved a crab-like body plan.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carcinisation in Lithodidae",
      "text": "The family Lithodidae, which includes king crabs, is a well-known example of carcinisation. These crabs evolved from hermit crab-like ancestors.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carcinisation in Porcellanidae",
      "text": "The family Porcellanidae, or porcelain crabs, also exhibits carcinisation. These crabs have a flattened body and a crab-like appearance, despite being more closely related to squat lobsters.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carcinisation in Hippoidea",
      "text": "The superfamily Hippoidea, which includes mole crabs, shows another instance of carcinisation. These crabs have adapted to a burrowing lifestyle with a crab-like form.",
      "date": "0000"
    }
  ]
}

