      "text": "On October 14, 1912, former U.S. President Theodore Roosevelt survived an assassination attempt by John Schrank, a former saloonkeeper, while campaigning for the presidency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Schrank's bullet lodged in Roosevelt's chest after penetrating Roosevelt's steel eyeglass case and passing through a 50-page-thick (single-folded) copy of his speech titled 'Progressive Cause Greater Than Any Individual', which he was carrying in his jacket pocket.",
      "date": "1912-10-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Schrank Disarmed and Captured",
      "text": "Immediately after the shooting, John Schrank was disarmed and captured by the crowd. He might have been lynched had Roosevelt not shouted for Schrank to remain unharmed. Roosevelt assured the crowd that he was alright, then instructed the police to take charge of Schrank and ensure he was not harmed.",
      "date": "1912-10-14"
    }
  ]
}

