{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Ancient Use of Poppies",
      "text": "Poppies, particularly Papaver somniferum, have been used since ancient times for their narcotic and medicinal properties. The opium extracted from poppies contains alkaloids such as morphine, which have been used as analgesics and narcotics.",
      "date": "-03000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Symbolism in Ancient Cultures",
      "text": "In ancient cultures, poppies were often associated with sleep and death due to their sedative properties. They were used in various rituals and as offerings to the dead.",
      "date": "-01500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Poppies in Greek and Roman Mythology",
      "text": "Poppies appear in Greek and Roman mythology, often linked to the gods of sleep and dreams, such as Hypnos and Morpheus.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Poppies in Medieval Medicine",
      "text": "During the medieval period, poppies continued to be used in medicine for their pain-relieving properties. They were a common ingredient in various medicinal concoctions.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Poppies in the Opium Trade",
      "text": "By the 17th century, poppies became central to the opium trade, particularly in Asia. Opium derived from poppies was a significant commodity in international trade.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Poppies in World War I",
      "text": "During World War I, poppies grew abundantly in the fields of Flanders, Belgium. The sight of these flowers inspired the poem 'In Flanders Fields' by John McCrae, leading to the poppy becoming a symbol of remembrance for soldiers who died in the war.",
      "date": "1915"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption as a Symbol of Remembrance",
      "text": "Following World War I, the poppy was adopted as a symbol of remembrance in the UK, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and other Commonwealth realms. It is worn on Remembrance Day to honor fallen soldiers.",
      "date": "1921"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
