      "text": "From 1942 to 1947, Franklin worked at the British Coal Utilisation Research Association, where she conducted research on the porosity of coal.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "PhD from Cambridge",
      "text": "In 1945, Rosalind Franklin received her PhD from Cambridge University for her work on the physical chemistry of solid organic colloids with special reference to coal and related materials.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Postdoctoral Research in Paris",
      "text": "In 1947, Franklin moved to Paris to work at the Laboratoire Central des Services Chimiques de l'État, where she learned X-ray diffraction techniques.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Research at King's College London",
      "text": "In 1951, Franklin joined King's College London as a research associate in John Randall's laboratory, where she worked on DNA structure.",
      "date": "1951"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Photograph 51",
      "text": "In May 1952, Franklin captured Photograph 51, an X-ray diffraction image of DNA that was critical to the discovery of its double helix structure.",
      "date": "1952-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Move to Birkbeck College",
      "text": "In 1953, Franklin moved to Birkbeck College, where she led pioneering work on the structure of viruses.",
      "date": "1953"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Research on Tobacco Mosaic Virus",
      "text": "Franklin's research at Birkbeck included significant contributions to understanding the structure of the tobacco mosaic virus, published in 1955.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Rosalind Franklin",
      "text": "Rosalind Franklin died on April 16, 1958, in Chelsea, London, England, from ovarian cancer.",
      "date": "1958-04-16"
    }
  ]
}

