      "headline": "Emergence of Blood Libel Accusations",
      "text": "The concept of blood libel, accusing Jews of murdering Christians to use their blood in religious rituals, first emerged in late antiquity as an accusation against early Christians in the Roman Empire, according to Tertullian.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Medieval Blood Libel Accusation",
      "text": "The first known medieval blood libel accusation against Jews occurred in England in the mid-1100s. This marked the beginning of a series of similar accusations across Europe.",
      "date": "1144"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of Blood Libel Accusations in Europe",
      "text": "Following the initial accusation in England, blood libel accusations spread to other parts of Europe, notably France and Germany, becoming a significant theme in the persecution of Jews.",
      "date": "1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blood Libel in France",
      "text": "In 1171, the first blood libel in France occurred in Blois, where over 30 Jews were burned at the stake following accusations of ritual murder.",
      "date": "1171"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blood Libel in Germany",
      "text": "In 1235, a blood libel case in Fulda, Germany, resulted in the massacre of 34 Jews after they were accused of murdering Christian children.",
      "date": "1235"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blood Libel in Poland",
      "text": "In 1399, a blood libel accusation in Poznań, Poland, led to the execution of several Jews, marking the spread of such accusations into Eastern Europe.",
      "date": "1399"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blood Libel in Italy",
      "text": "In 1475, the blood libel case of Simon of Trent in Italy led to the execution of 15 Jews and the canonization of the alleged victim by the Catholic Church.",
      "date": "1475"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Blood Libel in Russia",
      "text": "In 1911, the Beilis trial in Kiev, Russia, was a notable blood libel case where a Jewish factory worker, Menahem Mendel Beilis, was accused of ritual murder. He was eventually acquitted.",
      "date": "1911"
    }
  ]
}

