      "headline": "Founding of White Star Line",
      "text": "The White Star Line was founded in 1845 by John Pilkington and Henry Wilson. It was initially established to operate packet services between Britain and Australia.",
      "date": "1845"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acquisition by Thomas Ismay",
      "text": "In 1867, Thomas Henry Ismay acquired the White Star Line. He restructured the company and focused on transatlantic passenger services.",
      "date": "1867"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Oceanic Steam Navigation Company",
      "text": "In 1869, Thomas Ismay formed the Oceanic Steam Navigation Company, which became the parent company of the White Star Line.",
      "date": "1869"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Launch of the SS Oceanic",
      "text": "The SS Oceanic, the first ship of the new White Star Line fleet, was launched in 1870. It marked the beginning of the company's focus on luxury and comfort.",
      "date": "1870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the 'Big Four' ships",
      "text": "Between 1901 and 1907, White Star Line introduced the 'Big Four' ships: Celtic, Cedric, Baltic, and Adriatic. These ships were known for their size and luxury.",
      "date": "1901"
    },
    {
      "headline": "RMS Titanic Disaster",
      "text": "On April 15, 1912, the RMS Titanic, one of White Star Line's most famous ships, sank after hitting an iceberg during its maiden voyage, resulting in the loss of over 1,500 lives.",
      "date": "1912-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Merger with Cunard Line",
      "text": "In 1934, due to financial difficulties, the White Star Line merged with its rival, the Cunard Line, forming Cunard-White Star Ltd.",
      "date": "1934"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of White Star Line",
      "text": "The White Star Line name was officially dropped in 1950 when Cunard Line purchased the remaining shares and the company was fully absorbed.",
      "date": "1950"
    }
  ]
}

