      "headline": "First Mongol Invasion of Japan",
      "text": "In 1274, Kublai Khan launched the first Mongol invasion of Japan. The Yuan dynasty, with the help of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo, sent a fleet to invade the Japanese archipelago. The invasion was ultimately unsuccessful due to a combination of Japanese resistance and a typhoon that damaged the Mongol fleet.",
      "date": "1274"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Mongol Invasion of Japan",
      "text": "In 1281, Kublai Khan attempted a second invasion of Japan. This time, a larger fleet was assembled, consisting of forces from both the Yuan dynasty and the Korean kingdom of Goryeo. However, the invasion was thwarted by another typhoon, which destroyed much of the Mongol fleet, leading to the failure of the invasion.",
      "date": "1281"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kamikaze Typhoons",
      "text": "The two typhoons that struck during the Mongol invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281 are collectively referred to as 'kamikaze,' meaning 'divine wind.' These typhoons are credited with protecting Japan from Mongol conquest and have become a significant part of Japanese cultural and historical identity.",
      "date": "1281"
    }
  ]
}

