      "headline": "Beginning of the Jurassic Period",
      "text": "The Jurassic Period began approximately 201.4 million years ago, following the end of the Triassic Period. This marks the start of significant geological and biological changes.",
      "date": "-201400000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early Jurassic Epoch",
      "text": "The Early Jurassic Epoch spanned from about 201.4 million years ago to approximately 174.1 million years ago. It was characterized by a warm climate and the breakup of the supercontinent Pangaea.",
      "date": "-201400000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Middle Jurassic Epoch",
      "text": "The Middle Jurassic Epoch lasted from approximately 174.1 million years ago to about 163.5 million years ago. This period saw the further diversification of dinosaurs and the appearance of the first birds.",
      "date": "-174100000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Late Jurassic Epoch",
      "text": "The Late Jurassic Epoch occurred from around 163.5 million years ago to approximately 145 million years ago. It was a time of high sea levels and the dominance of large sauropod dinosaurs.",
      "date": "-163500000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Jurassic Period",
      "text": "The Jurassic Period ended approximately 145 million years ago, transitioning into the Cretaceous Period. This marked the continuation of dinosaur dominance and the rise of flowering plants.",
      "date": "-145000000"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Resistance to Siad Barre's Regime",
      "text": "During the 1980s, resistance to the military junta led by Siad Barre began to grow. Various armed rebel groups, including the Somali Salvation Democratic Front, the Somali National Movement, and the United Somali Congress, started to engage in combat against the Somali Armed Forces.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Somali National Movement Uprising",
      "text": "In 1988, the Somali National Movement (SNM) launched an uprising in the northwestern part of Somalia, marking the beginning of the Somaliland War of Independence.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Escalation of Civil War",
      "text": "From 1988 to 1990, the Somali Armed Forces engaged in combat against various armed rebel groups, leading to an escalation of the civil war.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Overthrow of Siad Barre",
      "text": "In January 1991, the clan-based armed opposition groups overthrew the government of Siad Barre, leading to the collapse of the central government.",
      "date": "1991-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Declaration of Somaliland Independence",
      "text": "On May 18, 1991, the northwestern part of Somalia declared independence as the Republic of Somaliland, although it has not been internationally recognized.",
      "date": "1991-05-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Operation Restore Hope",
      "text": "In December 1992, the United States led a humanitarian military intervention known as Operation Restore Hope, authorized by the United Nations to provide relief to the famine-stricken population.",
      "date": "1992-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Mogadishu",
      "text": "On October 3-4, 1993, the Battle of Mogadishu occurred, resulting in significant casualties for both U.S. forces and Somali militias. This event was later depicted in the film 'Black Hawk Down'.",
      "date": "1993-10-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Withdrawal of UN Forces",
      "text": "In March 1995, the United Nations withdrew its peacekeeping forces from Somalia after failing to restore order.",
      "date": "1995-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of the Islamic Courts Union",
      "text": "In 2006, the Islamic Courts Union (ICU) gained control over much of southern Somalia, including the capital Mogadishu, before being ousted by Ethiopian forces later that year.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Transitional Federal Government",
      "text": "In 2004, the Transitional Federal Government (TFG) was established in an attempt to restore national governance.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Al-Shabaab Insurgency",
      "text": "Since 2007, the militant group Al-Shabaab has been engaged in an insurgency against the Somali government and African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM) forces.",
      "date": "2007"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Federal Government of Somalia",
      "text": "In August 2012, the Federal Government of Somalia was established, marking the end of the transitional period and the beginning of a new phase in Somali governance.",
      "date": "2012-08"
    }
  ]
}

