      "headline": "Founding of the PKK",
      "text": "The Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK) was founded in Ziyaret, Lice, on November 27, 1978. The organization was established with the aim of creating an independent Kurdish state.",
      "date": "1978-11-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Beginning of Armed Conflict",
      "text": "In 1984, the PKK began its armed struggle against the Turkish state, marking the start of the Kurdish–Turkish conflict. The conflict has been characterized by guerrilla warfare and has resulted in significant casualties on both sides.",
      "date": "1984"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shift in PKK's Goals",
      "text": "During the 1990s, the PKK shifted its official platform from seeking an independent Kurdish state to seeking autonomy and increased political and cultural rights for Kurds within Turkey.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Capture of Abdullah Öcalan",
      "text": "On February 15, 1999, Abdullah Öcalan, the leader of the PKK, was captured by Turkish security forces in Nairobi, Kenya. His capture was a significant event in the history of the PKK and the Kurdish–Turkish conflict.",
      "date": "1999-02-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ceasefire and Peace Talks",
      "text": "In 2013, the PKK declared a ceasefire and entered into peace talks with the Turkish government. These talks aimed to resolve the ongoing conflict and address the demands for Kurdish rights.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Resumption of Conflict",
      "text": "In 2015, the ceasefire between the PKK and the Turkish government broke down, leading to a resumption of hostilities and increased violence in the region.",
      "date": "2015"
    }
  ]
}

