      "headline": "Formation of Coal Forests",
      "text": "During the late Carboniferous (Pennsylvanian) and Permian times, vast deposits of coal originated in former wetlands called coal forests. These forests covered much of the Earth's tropical land areas, providing the plant matter that would eventually transform into coal.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Carboniferous Period",
      "text": "Approximately 359 to 299 million years ago, during the Carboniferous Period, significant coal formation occurred. This era is known for the extensive development of coal forests, which laid down the plant material that would become coal.",
      "date": "-0359"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Permian Period",
      "text": "Following the Carboniferous, the Permian Period (approximately 299 to 252 million years ago) also contributed to coal formation. The conditions during this time continued to favor the accumulation and burial of plant material.",
      "date": "-0299"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Industrial Revolution",
      "text": "In the late 18th century, coal became a major energy source during the Industrial Revolution. Its use in steam engines and other industrial processes marked a significant shift in energy consumption and industrialization.",
      "date": "1760"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak Coal Production",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, coal production reached its peak as it became the dominant source of energy for electricity generation and industrial processes worldwide.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Coal Use",
      "text": "In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, coal use began to decline in many parts of the world due to environmental concerns and the rise of alternative energy sources such as natural gas, wind, and solar power.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

