{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Early Concepts of Integration",
      "text": "The concept of integration can be traced back to ancient civilizations, where methods for calculating areas and volumes were developed. Notably, the Egyptians and Babylonians used early forms of integration techniques.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Archimedes' Method of Exhaustion",
      "text": "Around 250 BCE, Archimedes developed the method of exhaustion, a technique to find areas and volumes by inscribing polygons within shapes and calculating their limits.",
      "date": "-0250"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Integral Calculus",
      "text": "In the late 17th century, Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz independently developed the fundamental theorem of calculus, establishing the relationship between differentiation and integration.",
      "date": "1670"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Newton's 'Principia Mathematica'",
      "text": "In 1687, Isaac Newton published 'Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica', which included his work on calculus, although he used geometric methods rather than the notation of calculus.",
      "date": "1687"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leibniz's Notation for Integration",
      "text": "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz introduced the integral symbol ∫ and the notation for integration in the late 17th century, which became standard in mathematical analysis.",
      "date": "1675"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bernhard Riemann's Contributions",
      "text": "In the 19th century, Bernhard Riemann developed the Riemann integral, providing a rigorous foundation for integration and extending its applicability.",
      "date": "1854"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Henri Lebesgue's Measure Theory",
      "text": "In 1902, Henri Lebesgue introduced the Lebesgue integral, which generalized the Riemann integral and allowed for the integration of a broader class of functions.",
      "date": "1902"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Modern Integration Theory",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, integration theory continued to evolve, with advancements in functional analysis and the development of various integral transforms.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
