{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Birth of John Stuart Mill",
      "text": "John Stuart Mill was born on May 20, 1806, in Pentonville, London, England.",
      "date": "1806-05-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Education and Early Life",
      "text": "From a young age, Mill was educated by his father, James Mill, with the assistance of Jeremy Bentham and Francis Place. He was taught Greek at the age of three and Latin at eight.",
      "date": "1810"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mental Crisis",
      "text": "In 1826, at the age of 20, Mill suffered a mental crisis, which led him to question the value of the utilitarian philosophy he had been taught.",
      "date": "1826"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'A System of Logic'",
      "text": "In 1843, Mill published 'A System of Logic', which established his reputation as a philosopher.",
      "date": "1843"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Principles of Political Economy'",
      "text": "In 1848, Mill published 'Principles of Political Economy', which became a standard textbook for economics.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Harriet Taylor",
      "text": "In 1851, Mill married Harriet Taylor after a long friendship and intellectual partnership.",
      "date": "1851"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'On Liberty'",
      "text": "In 1859, Mill published 'On Liberty', a work that argued for the importance of individual freedom and limited government intervention.",
      "date": "1859"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Utilitarianism'",
      "text": "In 1863, Mill published 'Utilitarianism', a defense of the ethical theory developed by Jeremy Bentham.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Election to Parliament",
      "text": "In 1865, Mill was elected as the Member of Parliament for Westminster, where he advocated for various social reforms.",
      "date": "1865"
