      "text": "Havana was founded by the Spanish on November 16, 1519. It was originally located on the southern coast of Cuba, but was moved to its present location on the northern coast in 1519.",
      "date": "1519-11-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Havana Becomes Capital",
      "text": "In 1607, Havana was declared the capital of Cuba, replacing Santiago de Cuba.",
      "date": "1607"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British Capture of Havana",
      "text": "On August 13, 1762, British forces captured Havana during the Seven Years' War. The city was returned to Spain in 1763 under the Treaty of Paris.",
      "date": "1762-08-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Havana's Fortifications",
      "text": "In 1774, the construction of the fortifications of Havana, including the Castillo del Morro, was completed to protect the city from future attacks.",
      "date": "1774"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Havana's Economic Boom",
      "text": "During the late 18th and early 19th centuries, Havana experienced an economic boom due to the sugar industry and became one of the wealthiest cities in the Americas.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cuban War of Independence",
      "text": "Havana played a significant role during the Cuban War of Independence from 1895 to 1898, which eventually led to Cuba's independence from Spain.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spanish-American War",
      "text": "In 1898, the explosion of the USS Maine in Havana Harbor precipitated the Spanish-American War, leading to the end of Spanish colonial rule in Cuba.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cuban Revolution",
      "text": "Havana was a focal point during the Cuban Revolution, which culminated in Fidel Castro's forces entering the city on January 8, 1959, marking the end of Batista's regime.",
      "date": "1959-01-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Havana's UNESCO World Heritage Status",
      "text": "In 1982, Old Havana and its fortifications were designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site due to their historical and architectural significance.",
      "date": "1982"
    }
  ]
}

