      "headline": "Emergence of Yaoi",
      "text": "The term 'yaoi' emerged in the late 1970s and early 1980s in Japan as a genre of manga that depicted homoerotic relationships between male characters. It was initially used to describe amateur manga, or doujinshi, that focused on romantic and sexual relationships between men.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Commercial BL Manga",
      "text": "In 1978, the first commercial Boys' Love (BL) manga, 'Kaze to Ki no Uta' by Keiko Takemiya, was published. This manga is often credited with laying the groundwork for the BL genre.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Popularity in the 1980s",
      "text": "During the 1980s, the BL genre gained popularity in Japan, with more manga artists and publishers producing works in this genre. The term 'yaoi' became widely used to describe these works.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Spread",
      "text": "In the 1990s, yaoi began to spread internationally, gaining a following outside of Japan. This was facilitated by fan translations and the internet, which allowed for greater access to BL works.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yaoi Conventions",
      "text": "The first Yaoi-Con, a convention dedicated to the yaoi genre, was held in San Francisco in 2001. This event marked a significant moment in the international recognition and celebration of the genre.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mainstream Recognition",
      "text": "By the 2000s, yaoi had gained mainstream recognition, with BL manga being officially licensed and published in various countries around the world.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Digital Distribution",
      "text": "In the 2010s, digital distribution platforms began to offer BL manga and anime, making it more accessible to a global audience and further increasing its popularity.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

