      "headline": "Draco's Legal Reforms",
      "text": "In the 7th century BC, the ancient Athenian lawmaker Draco is said to have made the distinction between murder and manslaughter, marking one of the earliest known legal differentiations between types of homicide.",
      "date": "-0620"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Common Law",
      "text": "Over the centuries, the concept of manslaughter evolved within the common law system, distinguishing it from murder based on the absence of premeditation or malice aforethought.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Manslaughter in English Law",
      "text": "By the 17th century, English law had clearly defined manslaughter as a separate offense from murder, focusing on the intent and circumstances surrounding the act.",
      "date": "1600"
