      "headline": "First Sweetest Day Celebration",
      "text": "The first Sweetest Day was celebrated on October 8, 1921, in Cleveland, Ohio. It was initiated by a committee of 12 confectioners led by Herbert Birch Kingston, a candy company employee, who wanted to bring happiness to orphans, shut-ins, and others who were often forgotten.",
      "date": "1921-10-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Sweetest Day",
      "text": "Throughout the 1920s and 1930s, Sweetest Day began to spread to other cities in the Midwest, including Detroit and Buffalo, as candy companies promoted the holiday.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sweetest Day Becomes a Regional Tradition",
      "text": "By the mid-20th century, Sweetest Day had become a well-established tradition in the Midwestern United States, celebrated on the third Saturday in October.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sweetest Day Criticism",
      "text": "Sweetest Day has been criticized as a 'Hallmark holiday' or a 'concocted promotion' created by the candy industry solely to increase sales of sweets.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sweetest Day Observance",
      "text": "Today, Sweetest Day is observed in parts of the Northeastern United States, in Arizona, and in Florida, in addition to the Midwest, as a day to share romantic deeds or expressions, and acts of charity and kindness.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

