      "text": "The Rosary, as a form of prayer, is believed to have been introduced in the 12th century. It was developed as a way for lay people to imitate the monastic practice of praying the Psalms.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Dominican Rosary",
      "text": "The Dominican Rosary, the form most commonly used today, was developed in the 15th century. It consists of 15 decades of Hail Marys, each preceded by an Our Father and followed by a Glory Be.",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Papal Endorsement",
      "text": "In 1569, Pope Pius V officially approved the Rosary in its current form, with the papal bull 'Consueverunt Romani Pontifices'.",
      "date": "1569"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Feast of Our Lady of the Rosary",
      "text": "In 1571, following the victory of the Battle of Lepanto, Pope Pius V established the Feast of Our Lady of the Rosary to be celebrated on October 7th.",
      "date": "1571"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Addition of the Luminous Mysteries",
      "text": "In 2002, Pope John Paul II added the Luminous Mysteries to the Rosary with the apostolic letter 'Rosarium Virginis Mariae'.",
      "date": "2002"
    }
  ]
}

