      "text": "Hippocrates, often referred to as the 'Father of Medicine', made observations about the spread of diseases and their relation to environmental factors around 400 BCE.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Graunt's Analysis",
      "text": "In 1662, John Graunt published 'Natural and Political Observations Made upon the Bills of Mortality', which is considered one of the first works of epidemiology, analyzing the mortality data of London.",
      "date": "1662"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Edward Jenner's Smallpox Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1796, Edward Jenner developed the smallpox vaccine, laying the groundwork for modern immunology and preventive medicine.",
      "date": "1796"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Snow's Cholera Study",
      "text": "In 1854, John Snow conducted a study on the cholera outbreak in London, identifying contaminated water as the source, which is considered a foundational event in the field of epidemiology.",
      "date": "1854"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the International Sanitary Conferences",
      "text": "Beginning in 1851, the International Sanitary Conferences were held to address the spread of infectious diseases across borders, marking the start of international cooperation in epidemiology.",
      "date": "1851"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Germ Theory",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch developed the germ theory of disease, which revolutionized the understanding of infectious diseases and their spread.",
      "date": "1880"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of the International Health Division of the Rockefeller Foundation",
      "text": "In 1913, the Rockefeller Foundation established its International Health Division, which played a significant role in the development of epidemiology as a scientific discipline.",
      "date": "1913"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Framingham Heart Study",
      "text": "The Framingham Heart Study began in 1948 in the United States, becoming one of the most important epidemiological studies in understanding cardiovascular disease.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eradication of Smallpox",
      "text": "In 1980, the World Health Organization declared smallpox eradicated, a major milestone achieved through global vaccination efforts and epidemiological tracking.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Human Genome Project Completion",
      "text": "Completed in 2003, the Human Genome Project provided a comprehensive map of human genes, greatly enhancing the field of genetic epidemiology.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "COVID-19 Pandemic",
      "text": "In 2019, the COVID-19 pandemic emerged, leading to unprecedented global epidemiological efforts to track and control the spread of the virus.",
      "date": "2019"
    }
  ]
}

