{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Mention in Greek Mythology",
      "text": "The Amazons are first mentioned in Greek mythology as a tribe of warrior women. They appear in various myths and legends, including the Labours of Heracles, the Argonautica, and the Iliad. These stories date back to ancient Greek times, with the earliest references appearing around the 8th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Labours of Heracles",
      "text": "One of the most famous myths involving the Amazons is the ninth labor of Heracles, which required him to obtain the girdle of Hippolyta, the queen of the Amazons. This myth is part of the Twelve Labours of Heracles, which were written down in various forms from the 6th century BCE onwards.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Argonautica",
      "text": "The Amazons appear in the Argonautica, an epic poem by Apollonius of Rhodes, written in the 3rd century BCE. In this story, the Argonauts encounter the Amazons during their quest for the Golden Fleece.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Iliad",
      "text": "In Homer's Iliad, the Amazons are mentioned as allies of the Trojans during the Trojan War. The Iliad is believed to have been composed around the 8th century BCE, although it was passed down orally before being written down.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Amazonomachy",
      "text": "The Amazonomachy, a popular theme in Greek art and literature, depicts battles between the Greeks and the Amazons. This theme was prevalent in Greek art from the 5th century BCE onwards, illustrating the cultural impact of the Amazon myths.",
      "date": "-0500"
    }
  ]
}

