      "text": "Throughout the 1970s, Israeli settlements expanded significantly in the West Bank, Gaza Strip, and Golan Heights, supported by the Israeli government.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Camp David Accords",
      "text": "In 1978, the Camp David Accords were signed, leading to the Egypt-Israel Peace Treaty. The accords included a framework for peace in the Middle East, addressing the Palestinian territories but not directly halting settlement activities.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Oslo Accords",
      "text": "In 1993, the Oslo Accords were signed between Israel and the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO), establishing a framework for future relations and the eventual establishment of Palestinian self-rule in parts of the West Bank and Gaza Strip. However, settlement expansion continued.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Court of Justice Advisory Opinion",
      "text": "In 2004, the International Court of Justice issued an advisory opinion stating that the Israeli settlements in the occupied territories were illegal under international law.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Court of Justice Ruling",
      "text": "In 2024, the International Court of Justice found in an advisory opinion that Israel's occupation was illegal and ruled that Israel had 'an obligation to cease immediately all new settlement activities and to evacuate all settlers' from the occupied territories.",
      "date": "2024"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Fossil Fuels",
      "text": "Fossil fuels began forming millions of years ago from the remains of prehistoric organisms. This process involves the decomposition of organic matter under heat and pressure over geological time scales.",
      "date": "-300000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Use of Coal",
      "text": "Coal has been used as a fuel since at least 1000 BCE in China. It was used for heating and cooking.",
      "date": "-1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Industrial Revolution and Fossil Fuels",
      "text": "During the late 18th century, the Industrial Revolution marked a significant increase in the use of fossil fuels, particularly coal, to power steam engines and factories.",
      "date": "1760"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Oil",
      "text": "In 1859, Edwin Drake drilled the first successful oil well in Titusville, Pennsylvania, marking the beginning of the modern petroleum industry.",
      "date": "1859"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Internal Combustion Engine",
      "text": "The late 19th century saw the development of the internal combustion engine, which increased the demand for petroleum products like gasoline.",
      "date": "1876"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Natural Gas as a Major Energy Source",
      "text": "In the 20th century, natural gas became a major energy source, used for heating, electricity generation, and as a chemical feedstock.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "OPEC Formation",
      "text": "The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was formed in 1960 to coordinate and unify petroleum policies among member countries.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Oil Crisis",
      "text": "The 1973 oil crisis was triggered by an OPEC oil embargo, which led to widespread fuel shortages and highlighted the world's dependence on fossil fuels.",
      "date": "1973"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Awareness of Climate Change",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, scientific consensus began to form around the impact of fossil fuel combustion on climate change, leading to international efforts to reduce carbon emissions.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Paris Agreement",
      "text": "In 2015, the Paris Agreement was adopted by 196 parties to limit global warming, emphasizing the need to reduce fossil fuel use.",
      "date": "2015"
    }
  ]
}

