      "headline": "Manuscript Given to Carthusian Monks",
      "text": "In 1605, François Annibal d'Estrées gave a manuscript containing the recipe for an 'Elixir of Long Life' to the Carthusian monks at the Grande Chartreuse monastery.",
      "date": "1605"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Production of Chartreuse Begins",
      "text": "In 1737, the Carthusian monks began producing the liqueur known as Chartreuse, following the instructions from the manuscript.",
      "date": "1737"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Green Chartreuse Introduced",
      "text": "In 1764, the monks refined the recipe to create what is now known as Green Chartreuse, a distinctive herbal liqueur.",
      "date": "1764"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yellow Chartreuse Introduced",
      "text": "In 1838, the Carthusian monks developed Yellow Chartreuse, a milder and sweeter version of the original liqueur.",
      "date": "1838"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Monks Expelled from France",
      "text": "In 1903, the French government nationalized the Chartreuse distillery, and the monks were expelled from France.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Production Moves to Spain",
      "text": "After their expulsion, the Carthusian monks moved production of Chartreuse to Tarragona, Spain.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Monks Return to France",
      "text": "In 1929, the Carthusian monks returned to France and resumed production of Chartreuse at their distillery in Voiron.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Distillery Moves to Aiguenoire",
      "text": "In 2017, the production of Chartreuse was moved to a new distillery in Aiguenoire, near the original monastery.",
      "date": "2017"
    }
  ]
}

