{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Earliest Known Satirical Work",
      "text": "The earliest known example of satire is the ancient Egyptian work 'The Satire of the Trades', dating back to around 2000 BCE. It humorously critiques various professions.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Greek and Roman Satire",
      "text": "Satire became a prominent genre in ancient Greece and Rome. Aristophanes, a Greek playwright, used satire in his plays such as 'Lysistrata' and 'The Clouds' around the 5th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Horace and Juvenal",
      "text": "In the 1st century BCE, Roman poets Horace and Juvenal developed the formal verse satire. Horace's 'Satires' and Juvenal's 'Satires' are key works that influenced the genre.",
      "date": "-0035"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Medieval Satire",
      "text": "During the Middle Ages, satire continued to evolve with works like Geoffrey Chaucer's 'The Canterbury Tales' (late 14th century), which used humor to critique social norms.",
      "date": "1387"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaissance Satire",
      "text": "The Renaissance period saw the rise of satirical works such as Erasmus's 'In Praise of Folly' (1511), which criticized the Catholic Church and societal norms.",
      "date": "1511"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jonathan Swift's 'A Modest Proposal'",
      "text": "In 1729, Jonathan Swift published 'A Modest Proposal', a satirical essay suggesting the eating of children to solve poverty, highlighting the British exploitation of Ireland.",
      "date": "1729"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Political Satire",
      "text": "The 18th century saw the rise of political satire with writers like Voltaire and his work 'Candide' (1759), which critiqued optimism and society.",
      "date": "1759"
    },
    {
      "headline": "19th Century Satire",
      "text": "The 19th century featured satirical works like Mark Twain's 'The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn' (1884), which addressed issues of race and identity in America.",
      "date": "1884"
    },
    {
