      "headline": "Relocation to Gandhinagar",
      "text": "In 1970, the capital of Gujarat was moved from Ahmedabad to Gandhinagar, and the Gujarat Legislative Assembly sessions began to be held in the new capital.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increase in Assembly Seats",
      "text": "The number of seats in the Gujarat Legislative Assembly was increased to 182 in 1975, allowing for greater representation of the state's population.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Reserved Constituencies",
      "text": "The Gujarat Legislative Assembly has 13 constituencies reserved for scheduled castes and 27 constituencies reserved for scheduled tribes, ensuring representation for these communities.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "BJP's First Majority",
      "text": "In 1995, the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) won a majority in the Gujarat Legislative Assembly for the first time, marking a significant political shift in the state.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Narendra Modi Becomes Chief Minister",
      "text": "In 2001, Narendra Modi was appointed as the Chief Minister of Gujarat, a position he held until 2014 when he became the Prime Minister of India.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Longest Serving Chief Minister",
      "text": "Narendra Modi became the longest-serving Chief Minister of Gujarat, serving from 2001 to 2014, overseeing significant economic and infrastructural development in the state.",
      "date": "2014"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recent Legislative Assembly Election",
      "text": "The most recent election for the Gujarat Legislative Assembly was held in December 2022, continuing the democratic process in the state.",
      "date": "2022-12"
