      "headline": "Early Weather Modification Attempts",
      "text": "In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, various attempts were made to modify weather, including rainmaking techniques using explosives and chemicals.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Cloud Seeding Experiment",
      "text": "In 1946, scientists Vincent J. Schaefer and Irving Langmuir conducted the first successful cloud seeding experiment using dry ice to create snow.",
      "date": "1946"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Silver Iodide for Cloud Seeding",
      "text": "In 1947, Bernard Vonnegut discovered that silver iodide could be used for cloud seeding, which became a common practice for weather modification.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Operation Popeye",
      "text": "From 1967 to 1972, the United States conducted Operation Popeye during the Vietnam War, using cloud seeding to extend the monsoon season and disrupt enemy supply lines.",
      "date": "1967"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Environmental Modification Convention",
      "text": "In 1977, the United Nations adopted the Environmental Modification Convention, which prohibits the use of environmental modification techniques for hostile purposes.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Project Stormfury",
      "text": "From 1962 to 1983, Project Stormfury was an attempt by the United States to weaken hurricanes through cloud seeding, but it was ultimately unsuccessful.",
      "date": "1962"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Weather Modification Practices",
      "text": "In the 21st century, weather modification continues to be used primarily for increasing precipitation and reducing hail damage, with countries like China and the United States actively engaging in cloud seeding.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

