      "headline": "Origins of Advaita Vedanta",
      "text": "Advaita Vedanta is rooted in the Upanishads, ancient Indian texts that form the philosophical basis of Hinduism. These texts date back to the late Vedic period, around 800-500 BCE.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gaudapada's Influence",
      "text": "Gaudapada, an early proponent of Advaita Vedanta, wrote the Mandukya Karika, a commentary on the Mandukya Upanishad, around the 6th century CE. His work laid the foundation for later Advaita Vedanta philosophy.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shankara's Contributions",
      "text": "Adi Shankaracharya, a key figure in Advaita Vedanta, was born around 788 CE. He traveled across India, establishing monasteries and writing extensive commentaries on the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita, and Brahma Sutras, solidifying the Advaita Vedanta doctrine.",
      "date": "0788"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shankara's Establishment of Monasteries",
      "text": "During his lifetime, Shankara established four major mathas (monasteries) in different regions of India, which played a crucial role in the spread and institutionalization of Advaita Vedanta.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Prakasatman's Vivarta Theory",
      "text": "In the 13th century, the scholar Prakasatman proposed the concept of vivarta, which describes the world as an unreal manifestation of Brahman, further developing the Advaita Vedanta philosophy.",
      "date": "1200"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the National Guard",
      "text": "The National Guard was officially established as a state-based military force that becomes part of the U.S. military's reserve components of the U.S. Army and the U.S. Air Force when activated for federal missions. It is composed of National Guard military members or units of each state and the territories of Guam, the Virgin Islands, Puerto Rico, and the District of Columbia, for a total of 54 separate organizations.",
      "date": "1636"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Militia Act of 1903",
      "text": "The Militia Act of 1903, also known as the Dick Act, was enacted to provide federal funding and regulation for the National Guard, establishing it as the primary organized reserve force for the U.S. Army.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "National Defense Act of 1916",
      "text": "The National Defense Act of 1916 further expanded the role of the National Guard, integrating it more closely with the regular Army and providing for its federalization during times of war or national emergency.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War I Mobilization",
      "text": "During World War I, the National Guard was mobilized for federal service, with units serving in Europe as part of the American Expeditionary Forces.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II Service",
      "text": "The National Guard was again federalized for service during World War II, with units participating in various campaigns across Europe, the Pacific, and North Africa.",
      "date": "1941"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Korean War Activation",
      "text": "National Guard units were activated for federal service during the Korean War, providing critical support and manpower to U.S. military operations.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vietnam War Involvement",
      "text": "During the Vietnam War, some National Guard units were activated for federal service, although the majority of the force remained stateside.",
      "date": "1960s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-9/11 Federalization",
      "text": "Following the September 11, 2001, terrorist attacks, the National Guard was heavily involved in both domestic security operations and overseas deployments as part of the Global War on Terror.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hurricane Katrina Response",
      "text": "In 2005, the National Guard played a crucial role in the response to Hurricane Katrina, providing disaster relief and security operations in the affected areas.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "COVID-19 Pandemic Response",
      "text": "During the COVID-19 pandemic, the National Guard was activated in various states to assist with logistics, testing, and vaccination efforts.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

