      "headline": "Establishment of the Legion of Honour",
      "text": "The Legion of Honour was established on May 19, 1802, by Napoleon Bonaparte as the highest French order of merit, both military and civil.",
      "date": "1802-05-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Awards of the Legion of Honour",
      "text": "The first awards of the Legion of Honour were made on July 14, 1804, during a ceremony at the Hôtel des Invalides in Paris.",
      "date": "1804-07-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reorganization under Louis XVIII",
      "text": "In 1814, after the fall of Napoleon, King Louis XVIII reorganized the Legion of Honour, retaining it as a national order.",
      "date": "1814"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Classes",
      "text": "In 1852, under Napoleon III, the Legion of Honour was expanded to include five classes: Chevalier, Officier, Commandeur, Grand Officier, and Grand Croix.",
      "date": "1852"
    },
