      "headline": "Publication of Goodhart's Article",
      "text": "In 1975, British economist Charles Goodhart published an article on monetary policy in the United Kingdom, where he expressed the core idea that would later become known as Goodhart's Law. He stated that 'any observed statistical regularity will tend to collapse once pressure is placed upon it for control purposes.'",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Goodhart's Law",
      "text": "The adage became widely known as Goodhart's Law, named after Charles Goodhart, as it gained recognition in the field of economics and beyond. The law highlights the problem of using statistical measures as targets for policy.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

