      "headline": "Julian Huxley coins 'transhumanism'",
      "text": "In 1957, Julian Huxley, a biologist and philosopher, coined the term 'transhumanism' in his essay 'Transhumanism', where he described the potential for humans to transcend their current limitations.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FM-2030's Vision",
      "text": "In the 1960s and 1970s, FM-2030 (born Fereidoun M. Esfandiary) became one of the first notable transhumanist philosophers, advocating for the use of technology to enhance human capabilities.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extropy Institute Founded",
      "text": "In 1988, Max More and Tom Morrow founded the Extropy Institute, which became a leading organization promoting transhumanist ideas and the concept of 'extropy', the opposite of entropy.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Transhumanist Declaration",
      "text": "In 1998, the Transhumanist Declaration was crafted, outlining the goals and principles of the transhumanist movement, emphasizing the ethical use of technology to enhance human life.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World Transhumanist Association Founded",
      "text": "In 1998, Nick Bostrom and David Pearce founded the World Transhumanist Association (WTA), which later became Humanity+, to promote the ethical use of technology to expand human capacities.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Humanity+ Rebranding",
      "text": "In 2008, the World Transhumanist Association rebranded itself as Humanity+, reflecting a broader focus on human enhancement and the ethical implications of emerging technologies.",
      "date": "2008"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transhumanist Party Founded",
      "text": "In 2014, Zoltan Istvan founded the Transhumanist Party in the United States, aiming to influence public policy in favor of transhumanist goals and technologies.",
      "date": "2014"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Beginnings of Modernism",
      "text": "Modernism began in the late 19th century as a response to the dramatic changes in society due to industrialization, urbanization, and the horrors of World War I. It marked a departure from traditional forms and embraced new ideas in art, literature, and culture.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Waste Land'",
      "text": "T.S. Eliot published 'The Waste Land' in 1922, a seminal modernist poem that reflected the disillusionment and fragmentation of post-war society.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bauhaus School Founded",
      "text": "In 1919, Walter Gropius founded the Bauhaus School in Germany, which became a leading institution for modernist architecture and design.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Exhibition of Cubism",
      "text": "Cubism, a revolutionary modernist art movement, was first exhibited in 1907, led by artists like Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque.",
      "date": "1907"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Ulysses'",
      "text": "James Joyce published 'Ulysses' in 1922, a landmark modernist novel known for its stream of consciousness technique and deep exploration of the human psyche.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Surrealist Manifesto Published",
      "text": "André Breton published the first Surrealist Manifesto in 1924, marking the formal beginning of the Surrealist movement, which sought to unleash the creative potential of the unconscious mind.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Style in Architecture",
      "text": "The International Style, characterized by an emphasis on volume over mass, balance rather than preconceived symmetry, and the expulsion of applied ornament, became prominent in the 1920s and 1930s.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modernism in Music",
      "text": "Composers like Igor Stravinsky and Arnold Schoenberg broke away from traditional tonality, leading to new forms of musical expression in the early 20th century.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abstract Expressionism",
      "text": "Emerging in the 1940s, Abstract Expressionism was a post-World War II art movement in American painting, with artists like Jackson Pollock and Mark Rothko leading the way.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Modernism",
      "text": "By the late 20th century, Modernism began to decline as Postmodernism emerged, challenging its principles and introducing new ideas and forms.",
      "date": "1970"
    }
  ]
}

