      "headline": "Development During World War II",
      "text": "During World War II, the German military explored the potential of railguns as anti-aircraft weapons, but the technology was not advanced enough for practical use.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Research",
      "text": "After World War II, research into railgun technology continued, primarily in the United States and the Soviet Union, focusing on military applications.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Electromagnetic Technology",
      "text": "In the 1970s and 1980s, advancements in electromagnetic technology and materials science led to renewed interest and research in railgun development.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "U.S. Navy Railgun Program",
      "text": "In the early 21st century, the U.S. Navy began a significant railgun development program, aiming to create a weapon capable of launching projectiles at Mach 7.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Successful Test Firing",
      "text": "In 2010, the U.S. Navy successfully test-fired a railgun prototype, achieving a muzzle velocity of 2,520 meters per second.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Development and Challenges",
      "text": "Throughout the 2010s, development continued with challenges in power supply, barrel durability, and integration into naval platforms.",
      "date": "2015"
    },
