    {
      "headline": "Introduction of ERP Concept",
      "text": "The concept of Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) began to take shape in the 1960s with the development of Material Requirements Planning (MRP) systems. These early systems were designed to manage manufacturing processes and inventory control.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of MRP II",
      "text": "In the 1980s, Manufacturing Resource Planning (MRP II) was developed, expanding the scope of MRP to include additional manufacturing processes and integrating financial and accounting systems.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coining of the Term ERP",
      "text": "The term 'Enterprise Resource Planning' (ERP) was coined in the early 1990s to describe the next generation of MRP II systems that integrated all facets of an enterprise into one comprehensive information system.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Cloud-based ERP",
      "text": "In the 2000s, cloud-based ERP systems began to emerge, offering businesses the ability to access ERP software over the internet, reducing the need for on-premises hardware and software.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Growth of Cloud ERP",
      "text": "Throughout the 2010s, cloud-based ERP systems gained popularity due to their flexibility, scalability, and cost-effectiveness, allowing businesses to access ERP functionalities from any location with internet access.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Invention of the Telharmonium",
      "text": "The Telharmonium, considered one of the first electronic musical instruments, was invented by Thaddeus Cahill in 1897. It used tonewheels to generate musical sounds as electrical signals.",
      "date": "1897"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Theremin",
      "text": "The Theremin, one of the earliest electronic instruments, was invented by Léon Theremin in 1920. It is unique in that it is played without physical contact.",
