{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Electric Motor",
      "text": "In 1821, Michael Faraday demonstrated the conversion of electrical energy into mechanical energy by creating the first simple electric motor.",
      "date": "1821"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the DC Motor",
      "text": "In 1832, William Sturgeon developed the first practical DC electric motor, which was capable of turning machinery.",
      "date": "1832"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Practical Electric Motor",
      "text": "In 1834, Thomas Davenport built the first practical electric motor, which he used to power a small model car on a circular track.",
      "date": "1834"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Invention of the Commutator",
      "text": "In 1837, Thomas Davenport patented the first electric motor with a commutator, which allowed for continuous rotation.",
      "date": "1837"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Induction Motor",
      "text": "In 1888, Nikola Tesla developed the first practical AC induction motor, which used alternating current to produce mechanical energy.",
      "date": "1888"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Universal Motor",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, the universal motor was developed, which could operate on either AC or DC power.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Electric Motor Technology",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, advancements in materials and electronics led to more efficient and powerful electric motors, including brushless DC motors and stepper motors.",
      "date": "1900-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Electric Motors",
      "text": "In the 21st century, electric motors have become integral to various applications, including electric vehicles, industrial machinery, and household appliances, with ongoing improvements in efficiency and performance.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

