      "headline": "Foundation of Yekaterinburg",
      "text": "Yekaterinburg was founded on November 18, 1723, as a factory-fortress to support the mining and metallurgical industries in the Ural region.",
      "date": "1723-11-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Yekaterinburg",
      "text": "The city was named after Saint Catherine, the namesake of Empress Catherine I of Russia, the wife of Peter the Great.",
      "date": "1723"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yekaterinburg becomes a city",
      "text": "In 1781, Yekaterinburg was granted city status by Catherine the Great.",
      "date": "1781"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of the Romanov family",
      "text": "On July 17, 1918, the last Russian Tsar, Nicholas II, and his family were executed by Bolsheviks in Yekaterinburg.",
      "date": "1918-07-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaming to Sverdlovsk",
      "text": "In 1924, Yekaterinburg was renamed Sverdlovsk in honor of the Bolshevik leader Yakov Sverdlov.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to original name",
      "text": "Following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the city reverted to its original name, Yekaterinburg, in 1991.",
      "date": "1991"
