      "headline": "Development of the AK-47",
      "text": "The development of the AK-47 began in 1945, shortly after World War II, when Mikhail Kalashnikov started working on a new rifle design for the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the AK-47",
      "text": "The AK-47 was officially adopted by the Soviet Armed Forces in 1949. It became the standard issue assault rifle for Soviet troops.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Production in the Soviet Union",
      "text": "The AK-47 and its variants were produced in large numbers in the Soviet Union, becoming a symbol of Soviet military power throughout the Cold War.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the AKM",
      "text": "In 1959, the AKM (Avtomat Kalashnikova Modernizirovanniy) was introduced as a modernized version of the AK-47, featuring improvements such as a lighter weight and increased accuracy.",
      "date": "1959"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Spread of Kalashnikov Rifles",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, Kalashnikov rifles spread globally, being produced under license or copied in many countries, especially those aligned with the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1960s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the AK-74",
      "text": "The AK-74 was introduced in 1974, featuring a smaller 5.45×39mm cartridge, which improved accuracy and reduced recoil compared to the AK-47.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Soviet Production",
      "text": "After the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991, production of Kalashnikov rifles continued in Russia and other former Soviet states, as well as in many other countries worldwide.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kalashnikov Concern",
      "text": "In 2013, the Kalashnikov Concern was formed as a merger of several Russian defense companies, becoming the main producer of Kalashnikov rifles.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Circulation",
      "text": "By the 21st century, it is estimated that over 72 million Kalashnikov rifles were in circulation globally, making it one of the most widely used firearms in the world.",
      "date": "2000s"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Esther Becomes Queen",
      "text": "Esther, originally named Hadassah, becomes the queen of Persia after King Ahasuerus chooses her as his wife. This event is set in the Achaemenid Empire, although the exact date is not specified in the biblical narrative.",
      "date": "-0480"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Haman's Plot Against the Jews",
      "text": "Haman, the grand vizier of King Ahasuerus, becomes offended by Mordecai, Esther's cousin and guardian, due to Mordecai's refusal to bow before him. Haman plots to kill all the Jews in Persia and convinces King Ahasuerus to permit this plan.",
      "date": "-0475"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Esther Foils Haman's Plan",
      "text": "Esther bravely reveals Haman's plot to King Ahasuerus, pleading for the safety of her people. The king is moved by Esther's courage and orders Haman to be executed. He also grants the Jews permission to defend themselves against their enemies.",
      "date": "-0475"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Purim",
      "text": "Following the events that led to the salvation of the Jews, the festival of Purim is established to commemorate Esther's bravery and the deliverance of the Jewish people. This celebration is marked annually on the 14th and 15th of the Hebrew month of Adar.",
