      "headline": "Formation of Gondwana",
      "text": "Gondwana began forming during the late Precambrian period, around 600 million years ago, as part of the supercontinent Pannotia.",
      "date": "-060000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gondwana as a Supercontinent",
      "text": "By the early Paleozoic era, around 500 million years ago, Gondwana had become a large supercontinent.",
      "date": "-050000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gondwana's Peak",
      "text": "During the late Paleozoic and early Mesozoic eras, approximately 300 million years ago, Gondwana was at its peak, covering much of the Southern Hemisphere.",
      "date": "-030000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Breakup of Gondwana Begins",
      "text": "The breakup of Gondwana began in the Jurassic period, around 180 million years ago, with the separation of Africa and South America.",
      "date": "-018000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Separation of India",
      "text": "Around 120 million years ago, during the Cretaceous period, India began to drift away from the rest of Gondwana.",
      "date": "-012000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australia and Antarctica Separate",
      "text": "Approximately 85 million years ago, Australia began to separate from Antarctica, continuing the breakup of Gondwana.",
      "date": "-008500000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Final Stages of Breakup",
      "text": "By around 30 million years ago, the breakup of Gondwana was largely complete, with the continents reaching their current positions.",
      "date": "-003000000"
    }
  ]
}

