    {
      "headline": "Coining of the Term 'Intersectionality'",
      "text": "In 1989, Kimberlé Crenshaw, a legal scholar, coined the term 'intersectionality' in her paper 'Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex'. She introduced the concept to address the ways in which various forms of social stratification, such as race, gender, and class, overlap and intersect.",
      "date": "1989"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Mapping the Margins'",
      "text": "In 1991, Kimberlé Crenshaw published 'Mapping the Margins: Intersectionality, Identity Politics, and Violence Against Women of Color'. This work further developed the concept of intersectionality, highlighting how women of color experience overlapping systems of oppression.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inclusion in Academic Discourse",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s, intersectionality gained traction in academic discourse, particularly within feminist theory, sociology, and critical race theory. Scholars began to apply the framework to various social issues, expanding its reach and influence.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Intersectionality in Legal Contexts",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, intersectionality began to be used in legal contexts to address cases where individuals faced discrimination based on multiple intersecting identities. This application highlighted the need for legal systems to recognize and address complex forms of discrimination.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Recognition and Application",
      "text": "By the 2010s, intersectionality had gained global recognition and was being applied in various fields, including public policy, education, and activism. It became a key framework for understanding and addressing systemic inequalities worldwide.",
      "date": "2010s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Intersectionality in Popular Culture",
      "text": "In the 2010s and 2020s, intersectionality became a popular topic in mainstream media and culture. It was increasingly referenced in discussions about social justice, diversity, and inclusion, reflecting its growing influence beyond academia.",
      "date": "2010s-2020s"
    }
  ]
}

