      "headline": "Ferdinand de Saussure's Lectures",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, Ferdinand de Saussure delivered a series of lectures at the University of Geneva, which laid the foundation for modern semiotics. His work was later published posthumously as 'Course in General Linguistics' in 1916.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Charles Sanders Peirce's Contributions",
      "text": "Charles Sanders Peirce, an American philosopher, developed a theory of signs that became a cornerstone of semiotics. His work spanned from the late 19th century into the early 20th century, with key publications in the 1930s.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Jakobson's Influence",
      "text": "Roman Jakobson, a Russian-American linguist and literary theorist, contributed significantly to the development of semiotics, particularly through his work on structural linguistics and communication theory in the mid-20th century.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roland Barthes' Seminal Works",
      "text": "In the 1960s, Roland Barthes, a French literary theorist, expanded the field of semiotics through his analysis of cultural phenomena and texts, notably in works like 'Mythologies' (1957) and 'Elements of Semiology' (1964).",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Umberto Eco's Theoretical Expansion",
      "text": "Umberto Eco, an Italian semiotician, novelist, and philosopher, published 'A Theory of Semiotics' in 1976, which further developed the theoretical framework of semiotics and its application to various forms of communication.",
      "date": "1976"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Establishment of the International Association for Semiotic Studies",
      "text": "In 1969, the International Association for Semiotic Studies (IASS) was founded to promote research in semiotics and facilitate communication among scholars in the field.",
      "date": "1969"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The First World Congress of Semiotics",
      "text": "The first World Congress of Semiotics was held in Milan, Italy, in 1974, marking a significant event in the formalization and international collaboration of semiotic studies.",
      "date": "1974"
    }
  ]
}

