      "text": "The Kumbh Mela is believed to have been celebrated for thousands of years, with its origins rooted in ancient Hindu mythology. The first recorded mention of the Kumbh Mela is found in the accounts of the Chinese traveler Xuanzang, who visited India in the 7th century.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kumbh Mela at Prayagraj",
      "text": "The Kumbh Mela at Prayagraj, one of the four major sites, is held every 12 years. This site is considered the most significant due to its location at the confluence of the Ganges, Yamuna, and the mythical Saraswati rivers.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kumbh Mela at Haridwar",
      "text": "The Kumbh Mela at Haridwar is celebrated every 12 years on the banks of the Ganges River. It is one of the four locations where the Kumbh Mela is traditionally held.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kumbh Mela at Nashik",
      "text": "The Kumbh Mela at Nashik is held every 12 years on the banks of the Godavari River. It is one of the four traditional sites for the Kumbh Mela.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kumbh Mela at Ujjain",
      "text": "The Kumbh Mela at Ujjain is celebrated every 12 years on the banks of the Shipra River. It is one of the four traditional sites for the Kumbh Mela.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Largest Gathering at Kumbh Mela",
      "text": "On 4 February 2019, the Kumbh Mela at Prayagraj witnessed the largest peaceful public gathering of humans ever recorded.",
      "date": "2019-02-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of Kumbh Mela at Bansberia Tribeni Sangam",
      "text": "In 2022, the Kumbh Mela was revived at Bansberia Tribeni Sangam in West Bengal at the confluence of the Hooghly and Saraswati rivers. This site had not hosted the Kumbh Mela for 700 years.",
      "date": "2022"
    }
  ]
}

