      "text": "The Lend-Lease Act was introduced as 'An Act to Promote the Defense of the United States' to Congress, marking the beginning of the policy that allowed the U.S. to supply Allied nations with essential materials during World War II.",
      "date": "1941-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Signing of the Lend-Lease Act",
      "text": "On March 11, 1941, President Franklin D. Roosevelt signed the Lend-Lease Act into law, authorizing the provision of military aid to foreign nations during World War II.",
      "date": "1941-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Lend-Lease Shipments",
      "text": "The first shipments under the Lend-Lease Act began in March 1941, providing critical support to the United Kingdom and other Allied nations.",
      "date": "1941-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extension to the Soviet Union",
      "text": "In October 1941, the Lend-Lease program was extended to include the Soviet Union, providing them with vital supplies to aid in their fight against Nazi Germany.",
      "date": "1941-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Lend-Lease Shipments",
      "text": "The peak of Lend-Lease shipments occurred in 1943, with the United States providing massive amounts of war materials to Allied nations.",
      "date": "1943"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Lend-Lease Shipments",
      "text": "On September 20, 1945, the Lend-Lease shipments officially ended, following the conclusion of World War II.",
      "date": "1945-09-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Termination of Lend-Lease Agreements",
      "text": "The formal termination of Lend-Lease agreements occurred in 1946, as the post-war recovery period began.",
      "date": "1946"
    }
  ]
}

