      "headline": "Emergence of Yamnaya Culture",
      "text": "The Yamnaya culture, also known as the Pit Grave culture, emerged around 3300 BC in the region between the Southern Bug, Dniester, and Ural rivers. It is characterized by its burial traditions and is considered a late Copper Age to early Bronze Age culture.",
      "date": "-3300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Characteristic Burial Tradition",
      "text": "The Yamnaya culture is named after its characteristic burial tradition. The term 'Yamnaya' is derived from the Russian adjective 'Я́мная', meaning 'related to pits'. This refers to their practice of burying the dead in tumuli (kurgans) with simple pit chambers.",
      "date": "-3300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Core Yamnaya Culture at Mikhaylovka",
      "text": "Research has identified Mikhaylovka, located in the lower Dnieper river region of Ukraine, as forming the core of the Yamnaya culture.",
      "date": "-3300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Yamnaya Culture",
      "text": "The Yamnaya culture persisted until around 2600 BC, marking the end of its influence in the region.",
      "date": "-2600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery by Vasily Gorodtsov",
      "text": "The Yamnaya culture was discovered by Vasily Gorodtsov following his archaeological excavations near the Donets River between 1901 and 1903.",
      "date": "1901"
    }
  ]
}

