      "headline": "Start of Construction",
      "text": "The construction of the Ellora Caves began around the year 600 AD. This period marks the beginning of the creation of one of the largest rock-cut temple complexes in the world.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Buddhist Caves",
      "text": "Between 600 and 730 AD, the Buddhist caves were constructed. These include caves 1 to 12, featuring monasteries and a large, multi-storied building with living quarters, sleeping quarters, kitchens, and other rooms.",
      "date": "0600-0730"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hindu Caves",
      "text": "The Hindu caves, numbered 13 to 29, were constructed between 600 and 870 AD. These caves include the famous Kailash temple (Cave 16), which is the largest single monolithic rock excavation in the world.",
      "date": "0600-0870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kailash Temple",
      "text": "The Kailash Temple, a chariot-shaped monument dedicated to the god Shiva, was constructed around 756-773 AD during the reign of the Rashtrakuta king Krishna I. It features intricate sculptures and relief panels depicting Hindu deities and epics.",
      "date": "0756-0773"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jain Caves",
      "text": "The Jain caves, numbered 30 to 34, were constructed between 800 and 1000 AD. These caves are smaller but feature detailed carvings and paintings, reflecting the Jain philosophy and tradition.",
      "date": "0800-1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UNESCO World Heritage Site",
      "text": "In 1983, the Ellora Caves were designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, recognizing their cultural and historical significance as a masterpiece of Indian rock-cut architecture.",
      "date": "1983"
    }
  ]
}

