      "text": "In 1171, Saladin abolished the Fatimid Caliphate of Egypt and established the Ayyubid Sultanate. He was proclaimed the first Sultan of Egypt by the Abbasid Caliphate.",
      "date": "1171"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Hattin",
      "text": "On July 4, 1187, Saladin achieved a decisive victory against the Crusaders at the Battle of Hattin, leading to the fall of most of the Crusader states, including the Kingdom of Jerusalem.",
      "date": "1187-07-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Saladin's Expansion",
      "text": "Following his victory at Hattin, Saladin expanded the Ayyubid Sultanate to encompass most of the Levant, Hijaz, Yemen, northern Nubia, Tarabulus, Cyrenaica, southern Anatolia, and northern Iraq.",
      "date": "1187"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Custodian of the Two Holy Mosques",
      "text": "By virtue of his sultanate including Hijaz, Saladin became the first ruler to be hailed as the Custodian of the Two Holy Mosques, a title held by all subsequent sultans of Egypt until the Ottoman conquest of 1517.",
      "date": "1187"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crusaders Reconquer Palestine Coast",
      "text": "In the 1190s, the Crusaders managed to reconquer the coast of Palestine, challenging the Ayyubid control in the region.",
      "date": "1190"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Ayyubid Rule",
      "text": "The Ayyubid dynasty's rule effectively ended with the Ottoman conquest of Egypt in 1517, which marked the end of the sultanate.",
      "date": "1517"
    }
  ]
}

