      "headline": "Introduction of Power over Ethernet",
      "text": "Power over Ethernet (PoE) technology was introduced to allow the transmission of electrical power along with data over standard Ethernet cables, enabling devices like wireless access points, IP cameras, and VoIP phones to receive power and data through a single cable.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IEEE 802.3af Standard Ratification",
      "text": "In June 2003, the IEEE 802.3af standard was ratified. This standard defined PoE technology, allowing up to 15.4 watts of DC power to be delivered to each device.",
      "date": "2003-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IEEE 802.3at Standard (PoE+) Ratification",
      "text": "In September 2009, the IEEE 802.3at standard, also known as PoE+, was ratified. This standard increased the power available to devices to 25.5 watts.",
      "date": "2009-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IEEE 802.3bt Standard Ratification",
      "text": "In September 2018, the IEEE 802.3bt standard was ratified. This standard further increased the power delivery capability of PoE to 71.3 watts, supporting more power-hungry devices.",
      "date": "2018-09"
    }
  ]
}

