      "headline": "Christianization of the Kingdom of Aksum",
      "text": "The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church traces its origins to the Christianization of the Kingdom of Aksum in 330 AD. This event marks the establishment of Christianity in Ethiopia, making it one of the earliest nations to officially adopt Christianity.",
      "date": "0330"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Council of Chalcedon",
      "text": "In 451 AD, the Council of Chalcedon took place, leading to a schism between the Oriental Orthodox Churches, including the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, and the Chalcedonian churches over theological differences.",
      "date": "0451"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Zagwe Dynasty and the Construction of Lalibela Churches",
      "text": "During the Zagwe Dynasty (circa 900-1270), the famous rock-hewn churches of Lalibela were constructed, which remain significant religious and cultural landmarks for the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Solomonic Dynasty Restoration",
      "text": "In 1270, the Solomonic Dynasty was restored in Ethiopia, which strengthened the influence and power of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church in the region.",
      "date": "1270"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding Member of the World Council of Churches",
      "text": "The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church became a founding member of the World Council of Churches in 1948, marking its active participation in the global ecumenical movement.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Autocephaly Granted",
      "text": "In 1959, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church was granted autocephaly by the Coptic Orthodox Church, allowing it to elect its own patriarch and operate independently.",
      "date": "1959"
    }
  ]
}

