      "headline": "Roman Conquest and Decline of Continental Celtic Languages",
      "text": "The Roman conquest of Celtic regions, beginning in the late 1st century BCE, led to the decline of Celtic languages on the continent as Latin became dominant.",
      "date": "-0050"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Old Irish Language Development",
      "text": "Old Irish, one of the earliest attested Celtic languages, began to be used around the 6th century CE.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Middle Welsh Language Emergence",
      "text": "Middle Welsh, a stage in the development of the Welsh language, was used from around the 12th to the 14th century.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Edward Lhuyd's Use of 'Celtic'",
      "text": "In 1707, Edward Lhuyd first used the term 'Celtic' to describe this group of languages, following the work of Paul-Yves Pezron.",
      "date": "1707"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival Movements",
      "text": "In the 19th and 20th centuries, there were significant revival movements for Celtic languages, particularly Irish, Welsh, and Scottish Gaelic.",
