      "headline": "Introduction of VC Theory",
      "text": "The statistical learning framework of VC (Vapnik–Chervonenkis) theory was proposed by Vladimir Vapnik and Alexey Chervonenkis in 1974. This theory laid the groundwork for the development of support vector machines.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Support Vector Machines",
      "text": "Support vector machines were developed at AT&T Bell Laboratories by Vladimir Vapnik and his colleagues in the early 1990s. This development was based on the principles of VC theory.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Key Paper",
      "text": "In 1992, a key paper on support vector machines was published by Vladimir Vapnik and his team, which detailed the theoretical foundations and practical applications of SVMs.",
      "date": "1992"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Soft Margin",
      "text": "In 1995, Corinna Cortes and Vladimir Vapnik introduced the concept of the soft margin, which allowed SVMs to handle non-linearly separable data by introducing a penalty for misclassification.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption of SVMs",
      "text": "By the late 1990s and early 2000s, support vector machines became one of the most popular and widely used machine learning algorithms for classification and regression tasks.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

