      "text": "The Typhoon-class submarines were designed and built by the Soviet Union for the Soviet Navy. They were developed to counter the United States Navy's Ohio-class submarines.",
      "date": "1970s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leonid Brezhnev's Speech",
      "text": "In 1974, General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev of the Communist Party mentioned a new type of nuclear ballistic missile submarine in a speech, which is often claimed to be the source of the NATO reporting name 'Typhoon'.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Typhoon-class Submarine Launched",
      "text": "The first Typhoon-class submarine, Dmitry Donskoy, was launched in 1980. It was the largest submarine ever built, with a submerged displacement of 48,000 tons.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commissioning of Typhoon-class Submarines",
      "text": "The Typhoon-class submarines were commissioned into the Soviet Navy throughout the 1980s, with a total of six submarines built.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Cold War",
      "text": "The end of the Cold War in the early 1990s led to a reduction in the number of active Typhoon-class submarines, as they were expensive to maintain.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decommissioning",
      "text": "Most of the Typhoon-class submarines were decommissioned by the early 2000s, with only one, Dmitry Donskoy, remaining in service for testing purposes.",
      "date": "2000s"
    }
  ]
}

