      "text": "On August 23, 1514, the Safavid Empire faced the Ottoman Empire in the Battle of Chaldiran. The Safavids were defeated, which limited their expansion westward.",
      "date": "1514-08-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Shah Abbas I",
      "text": "Shah Abbas I, also known as Abbas the Great, reigned from 1588 to 1629. He is credited with strengthening the Safavid Empire, moving the capital to Isfahan, and fostering trade and the arts.",
      "date": "1588"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Capital moved to Isfahan",
      "text": "In 1598, Shah Abbas I moved the capital of the Safavid Empire to Isfahan, which became a center of art, architecture, and commerce.",
      "date": "1598"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Safavid-Ottoman War (1623–1639)",
      "text": "The Safavid Empire engaged in a prolonged conflict with the Ottoman Empire from 1623 to 1639, which ended with the Treaty of Zuhab, establishing borders that largely remain today between Iran and Iraq.",
      "date": "1623"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Treaty of Zuhab",
      "text": "In 1639, the Treaty of Zuhab was signed, ending the Safavid-Ottoman War and establishing borders between the two empires.",
      "date": "1639"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of the Safavid Empire",
      "text": "The Safavid Empire began to decline in the late 17th century due to internal strife, weak leadership, and external pressures.",
      "date": "1694"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of the Safavid Empire",
      "text": "In 1736, the Safavid Empire came to an end when Nader Shah deposed the last Safavid ruler, marking the rise of the Afsharid dynasty.",
      "date": "1736"
    }
  ]
}

