      "headline": "Arrival of Slavs in the Balkans",
      "text": "The Slavs, ancestors of the Bosniaks, began settling in the Balkans during the 6th and 7th centuries.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Bosnian Kingdom",
      "text": "The medieval Bosnian state emerged as a distinct entity in the 12th century, with Ban Kulin ruling from 1180 to 1204.",
      "date": "1180"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ottoman Conquest of Bosnia",
      "text": "The Ottoman Empire conquered Bosnia in 1463, leading to significant cultural and religious changes, including the conversion of many Bosnians to Islam.",
      "date": "1463"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Austro-Hungarian Annexation",
      "text": "Bosnia and Herzegovina were annexed by Austria-Hungary in 1908, following the Congress of Berlin in 1878 which had placed the region under Austro-Hungarian administration.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Yugoslavia",
      "text": "After World War I, Bosnia became part of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats, and Slovenes, later known as Yugoslavia, in 1918.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II and the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia",
      "text": "During World War II, Bosnia was occupied by Axis powers. After the war, it became one of the six republics of the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia in 1945.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bosnian War and Independence",
      "text": "Following the breakup of Yugoslavia, Bosnia and Herzegovina declared independence in 1992, leading to the Bosnian War which lasted until 1995.",
      "date": "1992"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dayton Agreement",
      "text": "The Dayton Agreement was signed in December 1995, ending the Bosnian War and establishing the current political structure of Bosnia and Herzegovina.",
