      "headline": "First Observation by Jan Ingenhousz",
      "text": "In 1785, Jan Ingenhousz observed the irregular motion of coal dust particles on the surface of alcohol, which is an early observation of what would later be known as Brownian motion.",
      "date": "1785"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal Observation by Robert Brown",
      "text": "In 1827, the botanist Robert Brown observed the random movement of pollen grains in water under a microscope, which became known as Brownian motion.",
      "date": "1827"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Einstein's Theoretical Explanation",
      "text": "In 1905, Albert Einstein published a paper providing a theoretical explanation for Brownian motion, suggesting that the motion was due to the random collisions of molecules in the fluid.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Experimental Confirmation by Jean Perrin",
      "text": "In 1908, Jean Perrin conducted experiments that confirmed Einstein's theoretical predictions about Brownian motion, providing evidence for the existence of atoms and molecules.",
      "date": "1908"
    }
  ]
}

