    {
      "headline": "Start of Ideological Divergence",
      "text": "In the late 1950s, ideological differences began to emerge between the People's Republic of China (PRC) and the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR). These differences were rooted in their interpretations of Marxism-Leninism and were influenced by their respective geopolitical interests during the Cold War.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Khrushchev's De-Stalinization Speech",
      "text": "In February 1956, Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev delivered a secret speech denouncing Joseph Stalin's policies and initiating a process of de-Stalinization. This move was criticized by Chinese leader Mao Zedong, who saw it as a betrayal of Marxist-Leninist principles.",
      "date": "1956-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sino-Soviet Debates",
      "text": "Throughout the late 1950s and early 1960s, debates between China and the Soviet Union intensified over the interpretation of Marxism. Mao Zedong criticized the Soviet Union's policy of peaceful coexistence with the West, labeling it as revisionism.",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Withdrawal of Soviet Experts",
      "text": "In July 1960, the Soviet Union withdrew its experts and advisors from China, marking a significant step in the deterioration of Sino-Soviet relations.",
      "date": "1960-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Public Split",
      "text": "By 1961, the Sino-Soviet split became public as both nations openly criticized each other's policies. The split was marked by a series of polemics published in their respective media.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sino-Indian Border Conflict",
      "text": "In 1962, the Sino-Indian border conflict further strained Sino-Soviet relations, as the Soviet Union's growing ties with India were viewed with suspicion by China.",
      "date": "1962"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sino-Soviet Border Conflict",
      "text": "In March 1969, armed clashes occurred along the Sino-Soviet border, particularly at Zhenbao Island, escalating tensions between the two countries.",
      "date": "1969-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Normalization of Relations",
      "text": "In the late 1980s, efforts were made to normalize relations between China and the Soviet Union. This culminated in Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev's visit to Beijing in May 1989.",
      "date": "1989-05"
    }
  ]
}

