      "headline": "Development of FN 5.7×28mm",
      "text": "The FN 5.7×28mm cartridge was developed by FN Herstal in response to a NATO request for a replacement for the 9×19mm Parabellum cartridge. The development began in the late 1980s, aiming to create a small-caliber, high-velocity round suitable for personal defense weapons.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of FN P90",
      "text": "In 1990, FN Herstal introduced the FN P90, a personal defense weapon designed to use the new FN 5.7×28mm cartridge. The P90 was intended for military and law enforcement use, offering a compact and lightweight firearm with high-capacity magazines.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of FN Five-seven",
      "text": "The FN Five-seven pistol was introduced in 1998, designed to complement the FN P90 by using the same FN 5.7×28mm cartridge. This pistol was aimed at providing a sidearm option that shared ammunition with the P90.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "NATO Trials and Evaluation",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and early 2000s, the FN 5.7×28mm cartridge underwent various NATO trials and evaluations as a potential replacement for the 9×19mm Parabellum. However, NATO did not officially adopt the cartridge.",
      "date": "1990s-2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Civilian Market Introduction",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, FN Herstal began marketing the FN Five-seven pistol and FN 5.7×28mm ammunition to civilian shooters in the United States, expanding its availability beyond military and law enforcement.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increased Popularity",
      "text": "The FN 5.7×28mm cartridge gained popularity among civilian shooters and law enforcement agencies due to its high velocity, low recoil, and armor-piercing capabilities. This led to increased production and availability of firearms chambered in this caliber.",
      "date": "2010s"
    }
  ]
}

