      "headline": "Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation Issued",
      "text": "On September 22, 1862, President Abraham Lincoln issued the Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation, which declared that as of January 1, 1863, all slaves in Confederate states in rebellion against the Union would be freed.",
      "date": "1862-09-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emancipation Proclamation Issued",
      "text": "On January 1, 1863, President Abraham Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation, officially changing the legal status of more than 3.5 million enslaved African Americans in the Confederate states from enslaved to free.",
      "date": "1863-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on Union Army",
      "text": "The Emancipation Proclamation allowed for former slaves to be received into the armed service of the United States, significantly increasing the Union's manpower during the Civil War.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Reaction",
      "text": "The Emancipation Proclamation garnered international attention and support for the Union cause, particularly from countries that had abolished slavery, such as the United Kingdom and France.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Thirteenth Amendment Passed",
      "text": "On December 6, 1865, the Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution was ratified, abolishing slavery throughout the United States and legally reinforcing the Emancipation Proclamation.",
      "date": "1865-12-06"
    }
  ]
}

