      "headline": "First Medal of Honor",
      "text": "Butler received his first Medal of Honor for his actions during the occupation of Veracruz, Mexico, in 1914.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Medal of Honor",
      "text": "In 1915, Butler was awarded his second Medal of Honor for his leadership during the Battle of Fort Riviere in Haiti.",
      "date": "1915"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War I Service",
      "text": "During World War I, Butler was promoted to the rank of brigadier general and commanded a Marine brigade in France, although he did not see combat.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Director of Public Safety in Philadelphia",
      "text": "In 1924, Butler took a leave of absence from the Marine Corps to serve as the director of public safety in Philadelphia, tasked with enforcing Prohibition.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retirement from the Marine Corps",
      "text": "Butler retired from the Marine Corps on October 1, 1931, after 34 years of service.",
      "date": "1931-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'War Is a Racket'",
      "text": "In 1935, Butler published 'War Is a Racket', a book in which he criticized the military-industrial complex and the profit motives behind warfare.",
      "date": "1935"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Smedley Butler",
      "text": "Smedley Butler died on June 21, 1940, in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, at the age of 58.",
