      "headline": "Composition of the Early Upanishads",
      "text": "The early Upanishads, including the Brihadaranyaka and Chandogya Upanishads, were composed during the late Vedic period. These texts are among the oldest Upanishads and were composed around 800-500 BCE.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Middle Upanishads",
      "text": "The middle Upanishads, such as the Katha, Mundaka, and Taittiriya Upanishads, were composed during the 5th to 3rd centuries BCE. These texts continued to explore philosophical concepts and the nature of reality.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of Later Upanishads",
      "text": "The later Upanishads, including the Shvetashvatara and Maitri Upanishads, were composed around the 3rd century BCE to the early centuries CE. These texts reflect the influence of other philosophical traditions and the development of new religious ideas.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration into Vedic Canon",
      "text": "Over time, the Upanishads were integrated into the Vedic canon as the concluding part, known as Vedanta, meaning 'the end of the Vedas'. This integration solidified their importance in Hindu philosophy and religious practice.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on Hindu Philosophy",
      "text": "The Upanishads have had a profound influence on Hindu philosophy, particularly on the development of Vedanta, one of the six orthodox schools of Hindu philosophy. They continue to be studied and revered for their insights into the nature of reality and the self.",
      "date": "0200"
    }
  ]
}

