      "headline": "Establishment as a POW Camp",
      "text": "In 1940, Bergen-Belsen was established as a prisoner of war (POW) camp by the German military. It initially held Belgian and French prisoners.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to a Concentration Camp",
      "text": "In April 1943, parts of Bergen-Belsen were converted into a concentration camp. It was initially designated as an 'exchange camp' for Jewish hostages intended to be exchanged for German prisoners of war held overseas.",
      "date": "1943-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of the Camp",
      "text": "Throughout 1944, Bergen-Belsen was expanded to accommodate Jews from other concentration camps, including Auschwitz. The camp's population grew significantly during this period.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Liberation by British Forces",
      "text": "On April 15, 1945, Bergen-Belsen was liberated by British forces. At the time of liberation, the camp was in a state of chaos, with thousands of unburied bodies and severely ill prisoners.",
      "date": "1945-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftermath and Memorialization",
      "text": "After the liberation, the site was used as a displaced persons camp until 1950. In 1952, a memorial was established to commemorate the victims of Bergen-Belsen.",
      "date": "1952"
    }
  ]
}

