      "headline": "Formation of the Left Opposition",
      "text": "In 1923, Leon Trotsky and other Bolshevik leaders formed the Left Opposition within the Communist Party of the Soviet Union. This group opposed the policies of Joseph Stalin and the direction in which he was taking the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1923"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trotsky's Exile",
      "text": "In 1929, Leon Trotsky was expelled from the Soviet Union by Joseph Stalin. This marked a significant moment in the development of Trotskyism as Trotsky continued to promote his ideas from abroad.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of the Fourth International",
      "text": "In 1938, Leon Trotsky and his followers founded the Fourth International, an international organization aimed at promoting Trotskyist ideas and opposing both Stalinism and capitalism.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Assassination of Leon Trotsky",
      "text": "On August 21, 1940, Leon Trotsky was assassinated in Mexico City by Ramón Mercader, an agent of the Soviet NKVD. Trotsky's death was a significant blow to the Trotskyist movement.",
      "date": "1940-08-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Trotskyism",
      "text": "After World War II, Trotskyism continued to influence various socialist movements around the world, with different factions interpreting Trotsky's ideas in diverse ways.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trotskyism in the 1960s and 1970s",
      "text": "During the 1960s and 1970s, Trotskyism gained prominence in various countries, particularly in Europe and Latin America, as part of the broader New Left movement.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trotskyism Today",
      "text": "In the 21st century, Trotskyism remains a significant ideological current within the global socialist movement, with various parties and organizations continuing to advocate for Trotsky's ideas.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

