      "headline": "Introduction of Binomial Nomenclature",
      "text": "The system of binomial nomenclature was introduced by Carl Linnaeus in his work 'Species Plantarum', published in 1753. This marked the beginning of the formal system of naming species using two Latin names.",
      "date": "1753"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption in Zoology",
      "text": "The binomial nomenclature system was adopted for zoology with the publication of the 10th edition of 'Systema Naturae' by Carl Linnaeus in 1758. This work is considered the starting point for zoological nomenclature.",
      "date": "1758"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Code of Zoological Nomenclature",
      "text": "The International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN) was established to provide a set of rules for the naming of animals, ensuring consistency and stability in the use of binomial nomenclature.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Code of Botanical Nomenclature",
      "text": "The International Code of Botanical Nomenclature (ICBN) was established to govern the naming of plants, fungi, and algae, ensuring the consistent use of binomial nomenclature in botany.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
