      "headline": "Establishment of the French First Republic",
      "text": "The French First Republic was founded on 21 September 1792 during the French Revolution, marking the end of the monarchy in France.",
      "date": "1792-09-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of King Louis XVI",
      "text": "King Louis XVI was executed by guillotine on 21 January 1793, following his trial and conviction for high treason.",
      "date": "1793-01-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Terror",
      "text": "The Reign of Terror, a period of political purges and executions, began in September 1793 and lasted until July 1794, led by the Committee of Public Safety.",
      "date": "1793-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of Maximilien Robespierre",
      "text": "Maximilien Robespierre, a leading figure in the Reign of Terror, was executed on 28 July 1794, marking the end of the Terror.",
      "date": "1794-07-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Directory",
      "text": "The Directory, a five-member committee, was established on 2 November 1795, replacing the National Convention as the government of France.",
      "date": "1795-11-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coup of 18 Brumaire",
      "text": "Napoléon Bonaparte staged a coup on 9 November 1799 (18 Brumaire, Year VIII in the French Revolutionary Calendar), overthrowing the Directory and establishing the Consulate.",
      "date": "1799-11-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Napoléon Bonaparte becomes First Consul",
      "text": "Following the coup of 18 Brumaire, Napoléon Bonaparte became the First Consul of France, effectively gaining control of the government.",
      "date": "1799-11-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Declaration of the First Empire",
      "text": "The French First Republic officially ended with the declaration of the First Empire on 18 May 1804, when Napoléon Bonaparte declared himself Emperor of the French.",
      "date": "1804-05-18"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Early Attempts at Intravenous Therapy",
      "text": "Attempts at providing intravenous therapy have been recorded as early as the 1400s. These early experiments laid the groundwork for future developments in intravenous administration.",
      "date": "-1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Successful Blood Transfusion",
      "text": "In 1665, the first successful blood transfusion was performed by Richard Lower, who transfused blood from one dog to another.",
      "date": "1665"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Human Blood Transfusion",
      "text": "In 1667, Jean-Baptiste Denis, a French physician, performed the first human blood transfusion, transfusing blood from a sheep to a human.",
      "date": "1667"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Intravenous Techniques",
      "text": "In the 19th century, significant advancements were made in the development of techniques for safe and effective intravenous therapy, including the use of saline solutions.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Use of Intravenous Therapy",
      "text": "The practice of intravenous therapy became widespread in the 1900s after the development of techniques for safe, effective use. This included the standardization of saline solutions and the development of sterile techniques.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Plastic IV Bags",
      "text": "In 1970, the introduction of plastic IV bags revolutionized intravenous therapy by making it safer and more convenient compared to the previously used glass bottles.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Smart Infusion Pumps",
      "text": "In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, the development of smart infusion pumps improved the precision and safety of intravenous therapy by allowing for programmable dosing and monitoring.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

